{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'readlink -f $(command -v codex); ls task/resources/final; head -50 paper.sty; sed -n '\"'1,90p' source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py; sed -n '120,180p' scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json; rg -n 'internal|apply_patch' paper_strategy_agent_codex_*.log | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"/usr/local/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex/bin/codex.js\nhelm.json\nopenllm.json\nprotocol.json\n%%%% ICLR Macros (LaTex)\n%%%% Adapted by Hugo Larochelle from the NIPS stylefile Macros\n%%%% Style File\n%%%% Dec 12, 1990   Rev Aug 14, 1991; Sept, 1995; April, 1997; April, 1999; October 2014\n\n% This file can be used with Latex2e whether running in main mode, or\n% 2.09 compatibility mode.\n%\n% If using main mode, you need to include the commands\n%             \\documentclass{article}\n%             \\usepackage{iclr14submit_e,times}\n%\n\n% Change the overall width of the page.  If these parameters are\n%       changed, they will require corresponding changes in the\n%       maketitle section.\n%\n\\usepackage{eso-pic} % used by \\AddToShipoutPicture\n\\RequirePackage{fancyhdr}\n\\RequirePackage{natbib}\n\n% modification to natbib citations\n\\setcitestyle{authoryear,round,citesep={;},aysep={,},yysep={;}}\n\n\\renewcommand{\\topfraction}{0.95}   % let figure take up nearly whole page\n\\renewcommand{\\textfraction}{0.05}  % let figure take up nearly whole page\n\n% Define iclrfinal, set to true if iclrfinalcopy is defined\n\\newif\\ificlrfinal\n\\iclrfinalfalse\n\\def\\iclrfinalcopy{\\iclrfinaltrue}\n\\font\\iclrtenhv  = phvb at 8pt\n\n% Specify the dimensions of each page\n\n\\setlength{\\paperheight}{11in}\n\\setlength{\\paperwidth}{8.5in}\n\n\n\\oddsidemargin .5in    %   Note \\oddsidemargin = \\evensidemargin\n\\evensidemargin .5in\n\\marginparwidth 0.07 true in\n%\\marginparwidth 0.75 true in\n%\\topmargin 0 true pt           % Nominal distance from top of page to top of\n%\\topmargin 0.125in\n\\topmargin -0.625in\n\\addtolength{\\headsep}{0.25in}\n\\textheight 9.0 true in       % Height of text (including footnotes & figures)\n\\textwidth 5.5 true in        % Width of text line.\n\\widowpenalty=10000\n\"\"\"Deterministic counterfactual allocation solver; no training or external files.\n\nProtocol: handle(dict) -> finite JSON dict. Extra request/response keys are forbidden.\nEvery episode starts with reset, clearing all arrays, transfer buffers and RNG.\nSeeds are arbitrary nonnegative integers and initialize RNG only, not workloads.\n\nreset: {op:'reset', seed:int} -> {ok:true}.\nupload_start: {op, encoding:'base64', dtype:'<f8', shape:[models,tasks],\n byte_length:int, chunk_count:int, sha256:64-char hex, task_ids:[str,...]}\n -> {ok:true}. Models 2..100000, tasks 2..100, bytes exactly models*tasks*8,\n logical limit 16 MiB; chunk_count=ceil(bytes/1048576), in 1..16.\nupload_chunk: {op, index:int, data:base64 str} -> {ok:true}; contiguous zero-based\n indices, each decoded block <=1048576 bytes, all except last full-sized.\nupload_commit: {op} -> {ok:true}; checks digest, finite [0,1] scores and shape.\n All buffering is candidate-owned memory; no host filenames or shared files.\nsolve: {op, targets:[distinct row int,...], rules:['mean','median','borda'],\n cap_fractions:[float,float], tolerance:1e-10, work_seconds:positive float}\n -> {solutions:[{status:str,tasks:[distinct column int,...]},...]}. At most\n eight targets; decisions are target-major, then rule-major, then cap-major.\n A selected coordinate is set to score + fraction*(1-score), fraction in [0,1].\n Both fractions can be zero for no-update experiments. Costs are one per\n selected coordinate, divided by task count for reported investment.\n Input scores/caps round to 10 decimal places before evaluation. Mean and\n median use float64; Borda is average pairwise win credit (ties half), updated\n competitors included. Co-first allows an aggregate deficit <= tolerance.\n status is 'feasible' (tasks witness), 'unattainable' (tasks=[]; full caps\n cannot win), or 'unresolved' (tasks=[]; honest bounded solver uncertainty).\n No optimality or internal-state self-report is accepted. Evaluator independently\n checks witnesses, ranks, unattainability and controls; no answers are returned.\n After one solve, release {op:'release'} -> {ok:true} clears episode buffers.\n No additional response fields, logs on stdout, file paths, polling, retries,\n state transfers, candidate-chosen objectives or hidden telemetry are supported.\n Maximum response: 48 decisions with at most 100 integer indices each, <64 KiB.\n reset -> upload_start -> upload_chunk(s) -> upload_commit -> solve -> release.\n A method may replace the solver below while preserving this exact interface.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport base64\nimport hashlib\nimport itertools\nimport math\nimport random\nimport time\n\nimport numpy as np\n\n_state = {}\n\n\ndef _rank_totals(matrix):\n    result = np.zeros(matrix.shape[0], dtype=np.int64)\n    for column in matrix.T:\n        _, inverse, counts = np.unique(column, return_inverse=True, return_counts=True)\n        below = np.cumsum(counts) - counts\n        result += 2 * below[inverse] + counts[inverse] - 1\n    return result\n\n\ndef _answer(indices):\n    return {'status': 'feasible', 'tasks': sorted(int(column) for column in indices)}\n\n\ndef _exhaustive(original, caps, wins):\n    available = np.flatnonzero(caps > original).tolist()\n    for size in range(len(available) + 1):\n        for subset in itertools.combinations(available, size):\n            indices = list(subset)\n            if wins(indices):\n                return indices\n    return None\n\n\ndef _mean_budget(original, caps, wins):\n    order = np.argsort(-(caps - original), kind='stable')\n    for size in range(len(order) + 1):\n        selected = order[:size].tolist()\n        if wins(selected):\n            return selected\n    return None\n\n\ndef _odd_median_budget(original, caps, competitor, tolerance):\n    already_above = np.count_nonzero(original - competitor >= -tolerance)\n    promotable = np.flatnonzero(\n        (original - competitor < -tolerance) & (caps - competitor >= -tolerance)\n    )\n    needed = max(0, len(original) // 2 + 1 - already_above)\n    if needed > len(promotable):\n        return None\n    return promotable[:needed].tolist()\n  \"title\": \"Scientific Evidence Artifact Manifest\",\n  \"type\": \"object\"\n}\n37:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/sh -lc \\\"sed -n '543,565p' task/evaluate_final.py; sed -n '610,650p' task/evaluate_final.py; sed -n '910,958p' task/evaluate_final.py; sed -n '1,105p' source_evidence/downstream/program/src/METHOD.md; sed -n '1,100p' references.bib; jq '{status,outcome,correct,metrics,qualification,program_tree_sha256,results_tree_sha256}' ../final_evaluation/final_manifest.json; ls -la previous_paper aggregated extra_tasks 2>/dev/null; env | rg '\\\"'^PAPER_EDIT'\\\"'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"        for row in document['rows']:\\n            content_hash = raw_digest(row['raw'])\\n            if content_hash != row['raw_hash'] or content_hash in hashes:\\n                raise ValueError('normalized raw-content duplicate')\\n            hashes.add(content_hash)\\n            expected = [row['raw'][task] / document['scale'] for task in document['tasks']]\\n            if expected != row['scores']:\\n                raise ValueError('unaudited features')\\n        rows = document['rows'][:12] if smoke else document['rows']\\n        matrix = np.round(np.array([row['scores'] for row in rows], dtype='<f8'), 10)\\n        if not np.isfinite(matrix).all() or np.any(matrix < 0) or np.any(matrix > 1):\\n            raise ValueError('invalid source score')\\n        means = np.mean(matrix, axis=1)\\n        nonwinners = [int(index) for index in np.argsort(-means, kind='stable') if means[index] < means.max() - TOLERANCE]\\n        count = 1 if smoke else min(TARGET_COUNT, len(nonwinners))\\n        if count < 1:\\n            raise ValueError('missing target population')\\n        targets = [nonwinners[int(index)] for index in np.linspace(0, len(nonwinners) - 1, count, dtype=int)]\\n        boards[family] = {'matrix': matrix, 'targets': targets, 'tasks': document['tasks']}\\n    return boards\\n\\n\\ndef aggregate(matrix, rule):\\n        raise TimeoutError()\\n\\n\\ndef witness(decision, indices, *, exact=False, lower=0):\\n    return {'tasks': list(indices), 'attainable': True, 'exact': exact, 'lower': lower, 'upper': len(indices)}\\n\\n\\ndef baseline_decision(decision, name, rng, deadline):\\n    check_deadline(deadline)\\n    count = decision.count\\n    if decision.wins([]):\\n        return witness(decision, [], exact=True)\\n    if not decision.wins(list(range(count))):\\n        return {'tasks': [], 'attainable': False, 'exact': True, 'lower': count + 1, 'upper': count + 1}\\n    if name == 'none':\\n        return {'tasks': [], 'attainable': True, 'exact': False, 'lower': 0, 'upper': count + 1}\\n    if name == 'reference':\\n        if count <= 6:\\n            for size in range(1, count + 1):\\n                for subset in itertools.combinations(range(count), size):\\n                    check_deadline(deadline)\\n                    if decision.wins(list(subset)):\\n                        return witness(decision, subset, exact=True, lower=size)\\n        if decision.rule == 'mean':\\n            order = np.argsort(-(decision.caps - decision.initial), kind='stable').tolist()\\n            for size in range(1, count + 1):\\n                if decision.wins(order[:size]):\\n                    return witness(decision, order[:size], exact=True, lower=size)\\n        if decision.rule == 'median' and count % 2:\\n            threshold = decision.rival - TOLERANCE\\n            needed = max(0, count // 2 + 1 - int(np.sum(decision.initial >= threshold)))\\n            available = np.flatnonzero((decision.initial < threshold) & (decision.caps >= threshold)).tolist()\\n            selected = available[:needed]\\n            if len(selected) == needed and decision.wins(selected):\\n                return witness(decision, selected, exact=True, lower=needed)\\n        if decision.rule == 'borda':\\n            check_deadline(deadline)\\n            constraints = LinearConstraint(decision.coefficients, decision.base - decision.base[decision.target] - TOLERANCE, np.inf)\\n            result = milp(np.ones(count), integrality=np.ones(count), bounds=Bounds(0, 1), constraints=constraints,\\n                          options={'time_limit': max(.001, min(.15, deadline - time.monotonic())), 'mip_rel_gap': 0.0})\\n            lower = 0\\n            if status == 'feasible':\\n                valid = decision.verify_direct(indices)\\n                quality = 1 - len(indices) / matrix.shape[1] if valid else 0.\\n            elif status == 'unattainable':\\n                valid = not decision.verify_direct(list(range(matrix.shape[1])))\\n                quality = 1. if valid else 0.\\n            if episode['case'] == 'constant':\\n                valid = valid and status == 'feasible' and indices == []\\n            if episode['case'] == 'copy':\\n                expected_status = 'feasible' if decision.verify_direct([]) else 'unattainable'\\n                valid = valid and status == expected_status and indices == []\\n            if episode['case'] != 'original' and status == 'unresolved':\\n                controls_complete = False\\n            reference = baselines['methods'][3]['observations'][decision_index]\\n            if reference['exact'] and status == 'feasible' and len(indices) < reference['lower']:\\n                valid = False\\n            observations.append({'target': target, 'rule': rule, 'fraction': fraction, 'allocation': solution,\\n                                 'validated': valid, 'reference': reference,\\n                                 'baselines': {item['name']: item['observations'][decision_index] for item in baselines['methods'][:3]}})\\n            contributions.append(quality if valid else 0.)\\n            signature.append([status, len(indices)])\\n            sound = sound and valid\\n        require_operation_ack(call({'op': 'release'}))\\n        failed = False\\n        return {'score': sum(contributions) / expected, 'correct': sound and controls_complete,\\n                'complete': True, 'controls_completed': sound and controls_complete if episode['case'] != 'original' else True,\\n                'duration': time.monotonic() - begin, 'signature': signature,\\n                'observations': observations}\\n    except BaseException as error:\\n        primary = error\\n        raise\\n    finally:\\n        try:\\n            candidate.cleanup(failed=failed)\\n        except (RuntimeError, OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):\\n            WORKER_EVENTS.append({'seed': episode['seed'], 'component': 'candidate', 'category': 'cleanup_failure'})\\n            if primary is None:\\n                raise ProtocolError('candidate cleanup failure') from None\\n\\n\\ndef write_results(directory, score, correct, feasible, evidence=None):\\n    directory.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\\n    metric = 'combined_score' if PLANE == 'search' else 'final_score'\\n    (directory / 'metrics.json').write_bytes(json_line({metric: float(score)}))\\n    (directory / 'correct.json').write_bytes(json_line({'correct': bool(correct)}))\\n    if os.environ.get('AGENTIC_TASK_QUALIFICATION') == '1':\\n        (directory / 'qualification.json').write_bytes(json_line({'schema_version': 1, 'feasible': bool(feasible)}))\\n    if evidence is not None:\\n        encoded = json_line({'schema_version': 1, 'episodes': evidence})\\n# Certified cap-response allocation through the supplied RPC interface\\n\\n## Scope and assumptions\\n\\nThis candidate accepts only the starter's documented `handle(request)` protocol.\\nIt preserves reset/upload/commit/solve/release, chunk hashing and score validation,\\ntarget-major/rule-major/cap-major ordering, the three requested rules, two supplied\\nfractions, and the exact response schema. Diagnostics and certificates are not\\nadded to responses. There are no downloads, filenames supplied by the caller,\\nexternal services, or additional dependencies beyond the starter's NumPy.\\n\\nScores are rounded to ten decimal places on commit. Selecting task `j` changes\\nonly the target's score to `round(x_j + fraction * (1 - x_j), 10)`. Investment is\\nthe number of selected tasks; the caller may normalize it by the task count.\\nRivals' task scores stay fixed. For mean and ordinary median, their aggregate\\nscores therefore also stay fixed. Borda ranks are different: improvements to the\\ntarget reduce its opponents' pairwise credits, which this implementation includes.\\nThe only winning criterion is the supplied co-first tolerance, not an invented\\nstrict margin. Saturating a selected coordinate cannot hurt any of these rules:\\nmean and median are coordinatewise monotone, and every target/opponent Borda gap\\nis nondecreasing. Thus endpoint subsets suffice for minimum support.\\n\\nThe caps are hypothetical score-space responses, not a prediction about training\\nexamples, compute, or causal contamination effects. No transfer, forgetting,\\nuncertain response, or reacting rivals are modeled. Under a genuine upper-response\\nenvelope, an exact support minimum would lower-bound the number of affected tasks\\nfor a successful real intervention, not its training cost.\\n\\n## Exact mean certificate\\n\\nFor a budget `k`, sort achievable gains `caps - original` in descending order.\\nThe top `k` gains maximize the attainable sum over all subsets of that size.\\n`_mean_budget` returns the first winning sorted prefix. Each smaller prefix fails,\\ncertifying that no smaller subset can succeed in the mathematical score-space\\nmodel. The final mean is evaluated directly on the attacked vector rather than\\nrelying on a separately rounded cumulative-sum comparison.\\n\\n## Exact odd ordinary median certificate\\n\\nFor `T = 2h + 1`, success requires at least `h + 1` coordinates whose values pass\\nthe same score-minus-rival tolerance comparison used by the final witness check.\\nCount those already passing, then promote exactly the deficit from coordinates\\nwhose caps pass. Fewer changes leave too few passing coordinates. If there are\\nnot enough promotable coordinates, success is impossible.\\n\\n## Exact even ordinary median certificate\\n\\nFor `T = 2h`, ordinary median is the average of order statistics `h` and `h + 1`\\n(one-based), not either middle statistic alone. `_even_median_budget` enumerates\\neach possible lower middle endpoint `a` from original scores and caps. For this\\n`a`, it finds the least endpoint `b >= a` for which `(a + b) / 2` wins. Using\\nendpoint comparisons directly also avoids cancellation in a derived `2r - a`\\nthreshold near a tolerance boundary.\\n\\nIt is sufficient to have at least `h + 1` scores at least `a`, and at least `h`\\nscores at least `b`. Promotions are partitioned into shared (meet both deficits),\\nlow-only, and high-only categories. If the deficits are `L, H` and `S` shared\\npromotions exist, use `s = min(S, max(L, H))` shared promotions, then\\n`max(0, L-s)` low-only and `max(0, H-s)` high-only promotions. Check that each\\ncategory has sufficient supply. The size is minimal: any solution needs at least\\n`max(L, H)` changes and at least `L + H - S` changes, and this construction attains\\nthe larger bound whenever feasible.\\n\\nEvery feasible endpoint subset has an actual lower middle endpoint in this\\nenumeration. Its upper middle endpoint is at least the enumerated admissible `b`.\\nConsequently the best constructed solution is a global support minimum. Work is\\npolynomial in the task count, including all even task counts through 100.\\n\\nOn supplied matrices with at most six tasks, all three scalar cases are additionally\\ncross-checked against independent cardinality-ordered exhaustive subset search\\n(at most 64 endpoint subsets). These checks run inside future RPC solves; no\\nfixtures, tests, or candidate code were executed during preparation of this patch.\\nIf a discrepancy occurs, the exhaustive witness is used. Every scalar witness\\nis checked again with the actual aggregate and tolerance before returning it.\\n\\n## Borda compatibility and bounded search\\n\\nTask credits are represented in integer half-win units. For a column with a tied\\ngroup of size `c` and `l` strictly lower models, twice the credit is `2l + c - 1`.\\nTotals share denominator `2 * (models - 1) * tasks`. This avoids accumulating\\nfloating tie errors. The tolerance is converted to an integer deficit by ceiling.\\n\\nLet `delta_ij` be the increase in the target's twice-credit against opponent `i`\\non task `j`, and let `g_j = sum_i delta_ij`. After selecting a set, the pairwise\\naggregate gap against rival `i` improves by `sum_j (g_j + delta_ij)` in these units.\\nEach local delta is between zero and two. All infeasibility declarations are\\ntherefore certified by failure even when every task is selected.\\n\\nA descending-`g_j` prefix and deletion pass give a feasible incumbent. For bounded\\nimprovement search, rivals with deficit at most `D - 2U` can be omitted, where `D`\\nis the largest deficit and `U` the incumbent size: satisfying the largest deficit\\nwith at most `U` tasks implies the common gain is at least `D - 2U`, so omitted\\nrivals are automatically satisfied. Zero-gain tasks cannot change any rank gap.\\nIndividual-rival sorted-coefficient bounds give a cardinality lower bound.\\nBranch-and-bound then tries budgets from that bound upwards, pruning when a\\nrival's best available contributions cannot fill its remaining deficit. It is\\nbounded by the supplied deadline and an internal node limit. The current feasible\\nincumbent is returned if search is interrupted; Borda optimality is not claimed\\nin that case. Final witnesses are validated against all initially winning rivals,\\nnot just the search screening set. Initially nonwinning rivals cannot become\\nthreatening because every gap is monotone.\\n\\nAggregate caches are local to a solve call, and equal fractions reuse decisions.\\nThe shared remaining work budget is divided among remaining Borda decisions.\\nMean/median computations are bounded polynomial operations and do not need an\\n@Article{Verhagen2014VirtualCS,\\n author = {T. Verhagen and J. V. van Nes and F. Feldberg and W. van Dolen},\\n booktitle = {J. Comput. Mediat. Commun.},\\n journal = {J. Comput. Mediat. Commun.},\\n pages = {529-545},\\n title = {Virtual Customer Service Agents: Using Social Presence and Personalization to Shape Online Service Encounters},\\n volume = {19},\\n year = {2014}\\n}\\n\\n@Inproceedings{Gaertner2001DomainCI,\\n author = {W. Gaertner},\\n title = {Domain Conditions in Social Choice Theory: The existence of collective choice rules under exclusion conditions for finite sets of discrete alternatives},\\n year = {2001}\\n}\\n\\n@Book{Fish2023GenerativeSC,\\n author = {Sara Fish and Paul Gölz and David C. Parkes and Ariel D. Procaccia and Gili Rusak and Itai Shapira and M. Wüthrich},\\n booktitle = {ACM Conference on Economics and Computation},\\n journal = {Journal of the ACM},\\n pages = {1 - 52},\\n title = {Generative Social Choice},\\n volume = {73},\\n year = {2023}\\n}\\n\\n@Article{Li2019UsersIT,\\n author = {Jia Li and Xuan Liu and Ling Ma and Wei-Qiang Zhang},\\n booktitle = {Informatics for Health and Social Care},\\n journal = {Informatics for Health and Social Care},\\n pages = {298 - 312},\\n title = {Users’ intention to continue using social fitness-tracking apps: expectation confirmation theory and social comparison theory perspective},\\n volume = {44},\\n year = {2019}\\n}\\n\\n@Article{Martin1996AnAO,\\n author = {W. Martin and D. Shields and B. Tolwinski and Brian M. Kent},\\n journal = {Journal of Policy Modeling},\\n pages = {603-621},\\n title = {An application of social choice theory to U.S.D.A. forest service decision making},\\n volume = {18},\\n year = {1996}\\n}\\n\\n@Article{Mishra2023AIAA,\\n author = {A. Mishra},\\n booktitle = {Social Science Research Network},\\n journal = {ArXiv},\\n title = {AI Alignment and Social Choice: Fundamental Limitations and Policy Implications},\\n volume = {abs/2310.16048},\\n year = {2023}\\n}\\n\\n@Article{Dai2024MappingSC,\\n author = {Jessica Dai and Eve Fleisig},\\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\\n journal = {ArXiv},\\n title = {Mapping Social Choice Theory to RLHF},\\n volume = {abs/2404.13038},\\n year = {2024}\\n}\\n\\n@Article{Serrano2014EvaluatingSC,\\n author = {E. Serrano and Pablo Moncada and M. Garijo and C. Iglesias},\\n booktitle = {Information Sciences},\\n journal = {Inf. Sci.},\\n pages = {102-124},\\n title = {Evaluating social choice techniques into intelligent environments by agent based social simulation},\\n volume = {286},\\n year = {2014}\\n}\\n\\n@Article{Teixeira2020SimulatingCD,\\n author = {Miguel Teixeira and P. D’orey and Zafeiris Kokkinogenis},\\n booktitle = {Simulation modelling practice and theory},\\n journal = {Simul. Model. Pract. Theory},\\n title = {Simulating collective decision-making for autonomous vehicles coordination enabled by vehicular networks: A computational social choice perspective},\\n volume = {98},\\n year = {2020}\\n}\\n\\n@Article{Eckert2005BeliefMJ,\\n author = {D. Eckert and G. Pigozzi},\\n booktitle = {Belief Change in Rational Agents},\\n pages = {0},\\n title = {Belief merging, judgment aggregation and some links with social choice theory},\\n year = {2005}\\n}\\n\\n@Article{Manning2025GeneralSA,\\n author = {Benjamin S. Manning and J. Horton},\\n booktitle = {Social Science Research Network},\\n journal = {SSRN Electronic Journal},\\n title = {General Social Agents},\\n year = {2025}\\n}\\n\\n@Article{Ciná2016ProvingCT,\\n author = {Giovanni Ciná and Ulle Endriss},\\n{\\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"completed\\\",\\n  \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"qualified\\\",\\n  \\\"correct\\\": true,\\n  \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n    \\\"final_score\\\": 0.6633040935672518\\n  },\\n  \\\"qualification\\\": null,\\n  \\\"program_tree_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:256b61cd82cc14b3374da94e4e0df0a96c8ec3c725fd05f75ae62fcf844ed7cf\\\",\\n  \\\"results_tree_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:8857316184929a50ac41adaeea022fbb16c92f7f4359fcc8396005a09c073e5e\\\"\\n}\\n\",\"exit_code\":1,\"status\":\"failed\"}}\n49:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_31\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/sh -lc 'apply_patch() { codex --codex-run-as-apply-patch \\\"$(cat)\\\"; }\\napply_patch <<'\\\"'PATCH'\\n*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: paper_strategy.json\\n+{\\n+  \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ready\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"selected_prompt_family\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"paper_standalone\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"recommended_title\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"central_question\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"On fixed public multitask score tables, how does the smallest task subset needed to make a target co-first change with the aggregation rule and the allowed fraction of score headroom?\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"central_thesis\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"In the evaluated score tables, changing the response cap reverses mean-versus-median investment orderings for some targets and makes other promotions unattainable, so aggregation-rule resistance must be reported conditional on the cap and target score vector rather than as a universal ranking.\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"narrative_rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"This is a shared static score-space study with condition-specific evidence from HELM MMLU and the six-task Open LLM table, not two chronological experiments. Original final allocations contain independently bounded mean/median reversals in both settings, attainable and unattainable cases, and Borda comparisons. For HELM target row 10, mean/median costs are 9/12 tasks at full headroom and 23/12 at half headroom; for Open LLM row 1609 they are 2/3 and 4/3. These within-target comparisons support a precise conditional thesis without asserting attainable training responses or optimizer superiority. A broad solver-family story lacks ablations, a continuous-frontier story exceeds the two tested positive caps, and a search-improvement story contradicts the zero search delta. The frozen non-winning selection is still a defensible studied method: search selection and the independently completed final evaluation are different facts.\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"prior_paper_story_status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"rejected\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"edit_framing_decision\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Fresh framing, not an edit pass: no previous_paper manuscript, resolved edit-context files, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory was found. The required enum 'rejected' means no inherited manuscript story is adopted, not that an unseen paper was evaluated. Compare prospective cap frontiers, broad cross-setting solver improvements, and the measured cap-dependent budget story; select the last on direct evidence rather than retaining a memory-derived narrative.\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"staged_input_impact\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Direct inspection of source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py and METHOD.md establishes exact scalar allocation mechanisms, bounded rival-aware Borda search, hypothetical headroom responses, and a co-first criterion. These support a score-space budget study, not new training or architecture claims.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The selected staged program source has the same file SHA-256 as ../downstream/program/src/program.py. Selection metadata directly identifies the first recorded candidate; later gain-window and mixture-cut candidates are not the studied method. Different recorded directory-tree hashes belong to different artifact scopes and are not silently equated.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py supplies the original comparator implementation. Its matched search score is retained as a limited selection fact, not replaced by an invented final baseline result.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Final original-case records, read independently of downstream_study, change the emphasis from prospective frontiers to two measured positive cap levels, exact reference budgets, and cap-conditioned reversals. Permutation, constant-score, and zero-update cases are controls rather than extra datasets.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The available ICLR style is single-column with a 5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height. Its generic historical header does not authenticate an official 2026 release; the writer must verify the official venue files before final submission.\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"method_groups\\\\\\\": [\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Static capped-response promotion model: mean, ordinary median, and Borda\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"supporting\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Defines the common scientific object: choose a target-only task subset, set selected scores to x + rho(1-x), keep rival score coordinates fixed, and minimize subset size subject to co-first tolerance. The three rules expose distinct score and rank mechanisms under the same intervention.\\\\\\\"\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Frozen allocation solver: exact scalar budgets and bounded rival-aware Borda search\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"proposed\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"This is the selected configuration actually linked to final evidence. Explain sorted gains for mean, threshold counting for odd median, endpoint counting for even median, and integer Borda gaps with a verified feasible fallback. Proposed means studied configuration, not demonstrated superiority or a novelty claim.\\\\\\\"\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Original frozen comparator\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"baseline\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Retain the original baseline where evidence exists: study.baseline_score and study.search_score_delta establish a tied search comparison. There is no separately grounded final evaluation of that full comparator, so do not extend the comparison to final results.\\\\\\\"\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Evaluator-recorded no-update, random-order, and immediate-gain greedy policies\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"baseline\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The final episode records contain matched task witnesses and bounds for these policies. Use no-update to expose already-co-first targets under alternative rules and use greedy/random for bounded descriptive allocation comparisons. Do not assume the evaluator's greedy policy is the original frozen baseline without an explicit implementation match.\\\\\\\"\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Independent exact or bounded reference allocation\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"supporting\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Reference lower/upper bounds and independently checked witnesses establish what is minimal in each reported decision. In original cases the reference exact flags are true and feasible selected cardinalities match their bounds; this supports exactness for those observations, not a universal exact Borda algorithm.\\\\\\\"\\n+    }\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"results_focus\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Primary comparison: paired mean and ordinary-median minimum task budgets for the same target at rho=1 and rho=0.5, separately for HELM MMLU and Open LLM. Ground every point in final.evidence.episodes, filtering solution.cases to case=original and pairing by family, target, rule, fraction, and seed.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Show both measured reversals explicitly: HELM row 10 changes from mean/median 9/12 to 23/12 tasks; Open LLM row 1609 changes from 2/3 to 4/3. These are descriptive counterexamples to a cap-independent ordering, not estimates of how frequently reversal occurs in future populations.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Report all sampled targets, including zero-cost cases and unattainable cases. Target selection is by original mean nonwinner rank, not by being a nonwinner under every rule: HELM row 40 already needs zero median updates. Do not discard it or reinterpret zero investment as an intervention effect.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Reachability under a fixed cap across all three rules: at full headroom all eight evaluated targets per family are reachable; at half headroom HELM mean/median/Borda counts are 5/5/4 and Open LLM counts are 7/6/3. The fixed denominator is eight original targets per family, not repeated seeds or controls. Use final.evidence.episodes; never count an unattainable empty witness as zero cost.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Explain solver mechanism without a superiority narrative: on the evaluated original decisions, feasible selected cardinalities match independently exact reference bounds. Matched greedy excess-task examples can illustrate threshold plateaus, such as HELM row 28 at half cap, where median needs 21 tasks versus the recorded greedy 52. Random policies have only the recorded seed/order coverage; no broad statistical claim follows.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Use matched permutation, constant-score, and zero-update controls as implementation checks only. Keep their contributions out of the scientific budget and reachability denominators; synthetic constant tables are not public empirical evidence.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Keep selection and final evidence separate: study.* records a non-winning selection, three scored attempts, zero unscored attempts, and zero search-score difference from the original baseline. final.qualification.* independently reports completed and qualified. These facts neither establish solver improvement nor establish causal training efficacy, universal validity, or unseen-family generalization.\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"figure_plan\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Figure 1, early overview: public score table plus frozen target and rivals -> capped target-coordinate updates -> mean/ordinary median/Borda -> minimum selected-task count or unattainability. Make the counterfactual assumption and absence of model training instantly visible.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Figure 2, main paired comparison: a 2-by-2 scatter matrix, dataset by positive cap, with mean minimum task fraction on x, median minimum task fraction on y, and an equality diagonal. Plot all eight targets in each panel; handle unattainability in explicitly labeled marginal rails, not as a numeric cost. Highlight only the preidentified within-target reversal examples.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Figure 3, reachability comparison: two panels, one per dataset, with the three aggregation rules on x and the count of reachable targets out of eight on y, comparing full and half headroom. This brings Borda and lost attainability into the main paper without conflating them with finite-budget averages.\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"figure_specs\\\\\\\": [\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"score_space_overview\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"required\\\\\\\": true,\\n+      \\\\\\\"purpose\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Give a page-1-or-2 visual definition of the score-space intervention, the common comparison design, and the distinction between a finite minimum and unattainability; use equations and labels, not fabricated example scores.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"diagram\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_generator\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"generate-concept-figure\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"group_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"scientific_object\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"metric\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"minimum_selected_task_count_or_unattainability\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"split_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"aggregation_rule\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_span\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"full_width\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"target_width_fraction\\\\\\\": 1.0,\\n+      \\\\\\\"layout_rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"paper.sty sets a single 5.5-inch text column, so full width equals column width; there is no two-column spanning environment to exploit. Use one horizontal conceptual panel, at most five major blocks and three rule branches, about 5.5 by 2.3 inches plus caption, occupying about 0.30 body page. Render all labels at least 9 pt. A half-width diagram would make the intervention and noncausal caveat illegible.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"allow_progression\\\\\\\": false,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_panels\\\\\\\": 1,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\\\\\": 5,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_series_per_panel\\\\\\\": 3\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"paired_mean_median_budgets\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"required\\\\\\\": true,\\n+      \\\\\\\"purpose\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Expose the within-target cap-dependent change in mean/median budget ordering across the two evaluated score tables, retaining all finite and unattainable observations.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"paired_scatter\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_generator\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"matplotlib\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"group_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"response_cap\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"metric\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"independently_bounded_minimum_selected_task_fraction\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"split_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"dataset_and_response_cap\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_span\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"full_width\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"target_width_fraction\\\\\\\": 1.0,\\n+      \\\\\\\"layout_rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"At the inspected 5.5-inch single-column width, use four panels in two rows, never four across: about 5.5 by 3.8 inches plus caption, about 0.48 body page. Keep both numeric axes on [0,1], place unreachable states in separated labeled rails, and use at least 8.5-pt rendered ticks and 9-pt axis labels. Eight target points are observations, not eight colored categories or legend series. Use no more than four status encodings, annotate only the reversal exemplar in each dataset, and show multiplicity when finite points overlap. Repeated seeds must not be jittered into apparent independent target observations.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"allow_progression\\\\\\\": false,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_panels\\\\\\\": 4,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\\\\\": 4,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_series_per_panel\\\\\\\": 4\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"cap_conditioned_reachability\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"required\\\\\\\": true,\\n+      \\\\\\\"purpose\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare whether promotion is possible under each rule and cap, including Borda, using the same fixed target denominator in every panel.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"grouped_dot\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_generator\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"matplotlib\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"group_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"aggregation_rule\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"metric\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"reachable_target_count_out_of_eight\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"split_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"dataset\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_span\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"full_width\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"target_width_fraction\\\\\\\": 1.0,\\n+      \\\\\\\"layout_rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Use two side-by-side panels within the single 5.5-inch text column, three rule labels and two cap markers per panel, about 5.5 by 2.0 inches plus caption or 0.26 body page. Keep a common 0-to-8 count axis and one shared cap legend at 9 pt or larger. Do not add budget averages, optimizer scores, controls, or a second definition of success to this figure.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"allow_progression\\\\\\\": false,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_panels\\\\\\\": 2,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\\\\\": 3,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_series_per_panel\\\\\\\": 2\\n+    }\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"supplementary_plan\\\\\\\": {\\n+    \\\\\\\"purpose\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"After references, provide the exact derivations, complete paired allocation evidence, control results, and reproducibility/provenance details needed to audit the static study, without moving the central reversals, unattainability, or noncausal limitations out of the main body.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"planned_subsections\\\\\\\": [\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Endpoint reduction and scalar budget certificates\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Expand the monotonicity argument for endpoint subsets; derive the mean sorted-gain minimum, the odd-median threshold count, and the even-median two-order-statistic endpoint procedure. Use the selected program and METHOD.md as implementation evidence and the reference lower/upper fields in final episodes as measured checks. Separate code-level derivations from measured findings; do not invent exhaustive-test results or claim theoretical novelty from source inspection alone.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Rival-aware Borda constraints and bounded search\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document pairwise win credits with half-credit ties, target-induced rival aggregate changes, integer gap constraints, incumbent validation, and deadline fallback from the frozen selected source. Tabulate recorded independent reference lower/upper bounds, including whether they close; do not import later gain-window or mixture-cut implementations as evaluated ablations.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Complete paired allocation and comparator tables\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"List every original target/rule/cap record, task count, normalized cost, status, reference bounds, and available greedy/random witness costs, with seed-level records where policies differ. Retain already-co-first and unattainable cases. Derive all tables directly from final.evidence.episodes and preserve explicit episode/case/observation pointers. No d+1 sentinel may be printed as an investment.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Matched controls and repeated-execution coverage\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report permutation, constant-score, and zero-update cases separately from original tables, using recorded validation flags and failures. Explain that three seeds repeat the same fixed target instances; identical selected allocations indicate repeatability, not uncertainty about a model population. Replay is only present if actually recorded and has no statistical weight; do not invent a replay result when solution.replay is null.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.status\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.correct\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Public snapshots, normalization, and reproduction map\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document task/resources/final/helm.json and openllm.json plus protocol.json, source URLs and checksums embedded in row provenance, HELM release v1.13.0, the pinned Open LLM contents revision, score scaling, fixed target selection, hash/alias clustering, and acquisition dates. Distinguish local resource hashes, upstream byte hashes, and transfer hashes. Record source-data license ambiguity and reacquisition rather than assuming model/code licenses cover score redistribution. Map each planned figure/table to an extraction script, environment, command, and actual evidence pointers when implemented; script names in this strategy are plans, not existing deliverables.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Selection scope and independent final assessment\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"A short audit statement, not a search narrative: selection used three scored attempts and zero unscored attempts with no search-score improvement over the original baseline. Record the independent completed/qualified final outcome using final.qualification.*, not inferred from selection. Keep search and final weighted scores in separate scopes; there is no matched final original-baseline delta. Do not turn an absent diagnostic into a passed gate or infer a failure mechanism that an evaluator did not identify.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"study.selection_kind\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.investigation_outcome\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.search_score\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.baseline_score\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.search_score_delta\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.attempts.total\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.attempts.scored\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.attempts.unscored\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.status\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.correct\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"LLM Usage\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Disclose the roles evidenced by the supplied source/selection artifacts and this stage's recorded logs: research-idea assistance, candidate-code assistance where documented, reference-query planning, paper framing, and subsequent writing assistance if actually used. State that quantitative evidence is evaluator-produced and that no training intervention was performed. Verify precise human/LLM roles against available records rather than guessing model identities, manual effort, or validation activity.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"study.selection_kind\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      }\\n+    ]\\n+  },\\n+  \\\\\\\"forbidden_headings\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Search Progression\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Campaign Phases\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Milestones\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Timeline\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"First Success\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Experiment A / Experiment B\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Cycle-by-Cycle Improvements\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Winning Candidate\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Architecture Evolution\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"forbidden_phrases\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"we then tried\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"first valid result\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"subsequent improvements\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"later variants\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"next-best\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"search winner\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"state-of-the-art manipulation resistance\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"median is universally more robust\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Borda is universally safer\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"measured training-data cost\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"proved contamination effectiveness\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"continuous cap frontier\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"correctness rate\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"correct and eligible\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"eligible\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"combined_score\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"rubric\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"worker_01\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"worker_02\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"notes_for_writer\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Scope of this stage: strategy only. Do not create paper.tex or claims_grounding.json, invoke final-paper validation, generate figures, or run new candidate experiments in this stage. The paper_standalone family calls for thesis-first organization, not discovery order.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Body organization and inclusive page allocation: title/abstract 0.50 page; introduction including early overview 1.00; related work 0.50; problem and intervention assumptions 0.90; allocation methods and certificate reasoning 1.35; data and comparison protocol 0.80; results including both quantitative figures and compact evidence tables 2.45; discussion and limitations 0.90; conclusion 0.35. Total target is 8.75 body pages, excluding references and substantive supplementary material. The venue accepts only 8 to 9 body pages, with page 8 at least 75 percent full if the body stops there. These are layout budgets, not empirical measurements. Do not use spacing tricks or invented experiments to reach them.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Use the page budget for scientific depth: define why task-count support is an investment proxy; derive endpoint reduction and median plateaus; distinguish odd/even medians and updated-rival Borda; show an exact cap-reversal table with separate rows for the two caps; discuss finite costs versus impossibility and the mean-based target sampling asymmetry. Keep figures near the explanations they support. If the available material still cannot fill a substantive compliant body, report insufficiency rather than add filler.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Use reader-facing section names such as Problem and Intervention Model, Allocation Certificates, Public Score Tables and Comparison Protocol, Cap-Dependent Promotion Budgets, and Scope and Limitations. The main scientific comparisons are aggregation rules under common interventions; solver baselines establish allocation context and are not the thesis.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Define x in [0,1], selected subset S, c_j(rho)=round(x_j+rho(1-x_j),10), uniform investment |S|, and normalized investment |S|/d. Co-first permits an aggregate deficit at most 1e-10; it is not strict first place. Ordinary even median averages its two middle scores. Borda is average pairwise win credit with half credit for ties, and rivals' aggregates must be recomputed after target improvement. Fixed rival coordinates do not imply fixed Borda totals.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Use only the two evaluated positive response fractions, 1 and 0.5, for the empirical cap comparison. The zero fraction belongs to the no-update control. Endpoint monotonicity supplies a model-level argument, not evidence for continuous response curves, strict-margin sensitivity, empirical-cohort ceilings, composition resampling, or actual training effects. Those experiments are absent.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Evidence mapping: use final.evidence.episodes for every main numerical allocation, count, example, and quantitative figure; the payload is paper_data.json.final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes. Use its exact episode/case/observation locations in downstream claim mapping. Method derivations additionally cite source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py and METHOD.md; snapshot context additionally uses task/resources/final/{helm,openllm,protocol}.json. These file paths are supporting artifacts, not newly invented grounding-source IDs.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Private-only audit context: study.selection_kind is non_winner_candidate; study.investigation_outcome is completed_no_winner; study.search_score equals study.baseline_score at 0.7239766081871346; study.search_score_delta is 0; study.attempts.total/scored/unscored are 3/3/0. final.qualification.status/outcome/correct are completed/qualified/true. final.metric.final_score is 0.6633040935672518 in a separate weighted evaluation scope. Do not subtract final and search scores or expose their internal field labels as scientific metrics. No failed final qualification is reported in this run; do not invent one or turn search non-selection into a validity failure. Missing gate-level diagnostics remain unknown.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The final evidence source marks candidate diagnostics unverified. Rely on independently recorded validation checks and reference lower/upper bounds, not candidate self-reports. Exact Borda costs can be asserted for recorded decisions with matching feasible witness size and exact reference bounds; the selected Borda algorithm itself remains deadline-bounded. Qualification is not a guarantee of novelty, causal validity, generalization, or submission completeness.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The final resource context is 57 HELM subjects and six normalized Open LLM task columns; final populations contain 42 and 2129 rows respectively. Use frozen resource metadata to document these context facts, not the task brief's broader public-catalog totals. Do not treat the six aggregate columns as six collections of all their subtask scores, and do not include Raw columns as independent tasks. Target labels in observations are local row indices, not public model identifiers; recover display names from the matching frozen resource only when needed.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Uncertainty: retain seed-level results, but distinguish execution seeds from training runs or random population samples. Selected original allocations are identical across the three recorded seeds. Report that fact and exact integer budgets rather than fabricated confidence intervals or significance tests. Random-order comparator variability can be summarized only from actual recorded orders/seeds with its small coverage stated. The eight rank-stratified mean-nonwinner targets per family are not a random sample of all models. Do not promise bootstrap, unseen-family, or composition robustness analyses absent from the artifacts.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Essential limitations belong in the abstract/method and main discussion: assumed independent capped task responses, no measured tokens/data/dollars, no transfer or forgetting, stationary rival score vectors, uniform rather than task-specific cost, co-first rather than strict win, only two positive caps, normalization-dependent comparisons, target-selection asymmetry, and finite snapshot coverage. An unattainable observation means failure under the stated score cap, not a measured training loss or algorithmic invalidity.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Planned quantitative implementation: write a downstream extractor for final.evidence.episodes that emits tidy original records and separately marked controls, verifies pairing and reference bounds, and reproduces the stated examples/counts before plotting. Collapse identical selected records across seeds only with an explicit repeatability note. Use infinity/status for impossible promotion; never use the reference d+1 sentinel as a finite budget, never average infinities away, and never score an empty unattainable allocation as zero investment.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Figure export contract: quantitative plots must be source-generated as PDF plus a 300-DPI PNG, and LaTeX must use the PDF when available. Every figure targets the inspected full 5.5-inch text column; do not treat 0.48 text width as a conference column. Use no more than three panels per row and the explicit per-figure label/series budgets. If only raster is available, regenerate from source at at least 2400 px width for these full-width figures; never upscale an existing raster.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The single required overview uses generate-concept-figure through the later orchestrator, not an agent invocation in this stage. Keep editable diagram source; obtain a vector PDF from its native diagram export when available. If only raster export is available, enforce the full-width raster floor. The overview should depict a score table and a hypothetical intervention, not a neural architecture, training pipeline, discovery timeline, or fabricated benchmark values.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The supplied bibliography is not yet a sufficient read-and-verified literature basis. Inspection found many off-topic query matches; do not cite them to fill a related-work section. Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU and Brandt2016HandbookOC have exact task-allowed title matches in references_provenance.json, but their retrieved metadata does not demonstrate full-text reading. Any later use must verify and actually consult permitted source material. Missing benchmark-context references require safe exact-identifier retrieval and provenance before citation; do not invent citation keys or pretend this stage read those papers.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Respect the excluded-literature boundary: do not identify or search for the prohibited prior study, expand citation graphs, browse related/citing material, or infer its results. No external literature retrieval was performed in this framing stage. Future verification should use only explicitly task-allowed identifiers and stop/document any accidental prohibited exposure. Position the contribution as a reproducible conditional analysis, not a first-ever result unsupported by a safe literature review.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Apply VENUE.md: ICLR 2026, double-blind, no author identities, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, personal links, or internal absolute paths in the manuscript. Verify official template provenance separately from the observed geometry. Include the grounded LLM Usage section after references, and satisfy any verified current venue-required checklist rather than inventing a NeurIPS checklist for ICLR.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Reproducibility is a remaining deliverable, not an accomplished claim: selected solver source and frozen input/evaluator artifacts exist, but paper figure/table scripts, a clean reader-facing reproduction command map, verified paper-level citations, and authenticated venue files are not yet supplied by this strategy. Reuse recorded source URLs, acquisition dates, seeds, and byte checksums; do not invent measured CPU time from resource estimates or episode durations. Preserve the original baseline comparison where supported, and explicitly omit any unmatched final full-program baseline comparison.\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"failure_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\n+}\\n*** Add File: paper_strategy.md\\n+# Paper strategy: cap-dependent task budgets\\n+\\n+**Status:** ready for the \\\"'`paper_standalone` family. This is a framing plan, not a manuscript or a statement that final submission requirements have already been met.\\n+\\n+**Provisional title:** Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion.\\n+\\n+## Central question and thesis\\n+\\n+**Question.** On fixed public multitask score tables, how does the smallest task subset needed to make a target co-first change with the aggregation rule and the allowed fraction of score headroom?\\n+\\n+**Thesis.** In the evaluated score tables, changing the response cap reverses mean-versus-median investment orderings for some targets and makes other promotions unattainable. Aggregation-rule resistance must therefore be reported conditional on the cap and target score vector, rather than as a universal ranking.\\n+\\n+Present HELM MMLU and the six-task Open LLM table as two settings of one static intervention model. Do not organize them as chronological experiments. The contribution is a conditional, reproducible score-space analysis with explicit allocation certificates and failure cases, not a training method, an architecture, or a demonstrated optimizer improvement.\\n+\\n+## Framing decision and alternatives\\n+\\n+This is not an edit pass. No seeded prior manuscript, resolved edit-context files, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` were found. The JSON enum `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` means no inherited manuscript story is adopted; it does not mean an unseen manuscript was assessed.\\n+\\n+The selected source and original comparator were inspected directly. The selected staged `program.py` matches the downstream source file by SHA-256. Selection metadata identifies the first candidate, not either later refinement. Recorded directory-tree hashes differ across artifact scopes; do not equate them based on a matching source file.\\n+\\n+Three candidate stories were considered:\\n+\\n+- **Prospective cap frontiers:** preserve the earlier conceptual direction, but narrow its language. Only two positive caps were evaluated; continuous frontiers, empirical response envelopes, strict-margin sensitivity, and composition resampling are not completed evidence.\\n+- **Broad cross-setting solver improvements:** reject as the center. There are no grounded ablations attributing gains to the later gain-window or mixture-cut variants, and search scores tie the original comparator.\\n+- **Measured cap-dependent promotion budgets:** select. The same-target rule comparisons directly demonstrate reversals in both score tables, while reachability and independent bounds make the limitations inspectable.\\n+\\n+This choice beats a campaign diary because it answers the original scientific question with controlled, interpretable comparisons. Neither a positive search winner nor model training is needed for this bounded study.\\n+\\n+## Independent final assessment and evidence hierarchy\\n+\\n+Read final assessment independently of selection. The following are **private audit facts**, not names for visible scientific metrics:\\n+\\n+- `study.selection_kind = non_winner_candidate`; `study.investigation_outcome = completed_no_winner`.\\n+- `study.search_score = study.baseline_score = 0.7239766081871346`; `study.search_score_delta = 0`.\\n+- `study.attempts.total/scored/unscored = 3/3/0`. Unscored attempts would be missing performance evidence, not measured losses.\\n+- `final.qualification.status/outcome/correct = completed/qualified/true`.\\n+- `final.metric.final_score = 0.6633040935672518` belongs to a separate weighted evaluation scope. It is not a matched change from the search score.\\n+\\n+No failed final qualification is reported in this run. Do not invent one, reinterpret non-selection as a validity failure, or treat passing assessment as proof of generalization or scientific novelty. Missing criterion diagnostics remain unknown. Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified; use recorded independent validation and reference bounds instead of self-report.\\n+\\n+Main quantitative grounding is `final.evidence.episodes`, whose payload is `paper_data.json.final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes`. Filter `solution.cases` to `case = original`, then pair by family, target, rule, fraction, and seed. Retain exact episode/case/observation pointers for downstream claim mapping. The method files and `task/resources/final/` files support implementation and provenance explanations; their paths are not invented grounding IDs.\\n+\\n+## Scientific definitions and scope\\n+\\n+For normalized target scores `x` and a selected task subset `S`, selecting coordinate `j` changes it to `round(x_j + rho(1-x_j), 10)`. Other target coordinates and every rival score coordinate remain fixed. Investment is the uniform task count `|S|`, optionally divided by task count `d` for cross-setting display. The empirical positive caps are `rho = 1` and `rho = 0.5`; zero belongs to the no-update control.\\n+\\n+Promotion means **co-first** within aggregate tolerance `1e-10`, not strict first place. The ordinary even median averages its two central values. Borda uses average pairwise win credit with half credit for ties: changing the target can change rivals'\\\"' Borda totals despite fixed rival task scores. Recompute those totals.\\n+\\n+This is a hypothetical score-response model. It does not measure training examples, tokens, money, compute, contamination efficacy, transfer, forgetting, or rivals' strategic responses. Only under a genuine upper-response envelope could an exact support minimum lower-bound affected tasks for a successful intervention; no evidence here calibrates such an envelope to real training.\\n+\\n+The frozen final context contains 57 HELM subjects and six normalized Open LLM columns, with 42 and 2129 rows respectively. These are resource-context facts, not the broader public-catalog totals from the task brief. Do not mix Raw columns into the six-task analysis or reinterpret their aggregate columns as all underlying subtasks. Target row numbers are local identifiers, not model names.\\n+\\n+## Narrow method groups\\n+\\n+1. **Static capped-response model, supporting:** the shared intervention and the three aggregation rules define the comparison.\\n+2. **Frozen allocation solver, studied/proposed:** sorted gains for mean, threshold counting for odd median, endpoint counting for even median, and bounded rival-aware integer Borda search with verified fallback. The label proposed does not assert novelty or superiority.\\n+3. **Original frozen comparator, baseline:** preserve its tied search comparison using \\\"'`study.baseline_score` and `study.search_score_delta`. No separate matched final evaluation of that full implementation is grounded.\\n+4. **Evaluator-recorded policies, baselines:** no update, random task order, and immediate-gain greedy. These have matched final allocation records; do not silently relabel the evaluator'\\\"'s greedy policy as the original frozen comparator.\\n+5. **Independent budget reference, supporting:** exact flags and lower/upper bounds certify individual reported costs. Finite selected allocations match exact reference cardinalities in the original records. This is not a proof that the deadline-bounded Borda algorithm is exact on every input.\\n+\\n+Exclude later gain-window and mixture-cut branches from the method narrative. They are not ablations, and their names are not evidence of an improved mechanism. Do not introduce unmeasured architecture shifts or training procedures.\\n+\\n+## Results that carry the argument\\n+\\n+### Cap-conditioned budget ordering\\n+\\n+Put this small table in the main body; each entry is a task count from \\\"'`final.evidence.episodes`, not a training cost:\\n+\\n+| Score table | Local target row | Headroom fraction | Mean | Median | Borda |\\n+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n+| HELM | 10 | 1 | 9 | 12 | 34 |\\n+| HELM | 10 | 0.5 | 23 | 12 | 39 |\\n+| Open LLM | 1609 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 5 |\\n+| Open LLM | 1609 | 0.5 | 4 | 3 | 5 |\\n+\\n+The within-target mean/median ordering reverses in each setting. These are counterexamples to an unconditional ordering, not estimates of reversal frequency in future model populations. Explain them using cumulative score gains versus order-statistic thresholds, not an untested causal account of developer behavior.\\n+\\n+Show all sampled targets in the main comparison, not only these examples. Targets were selected as nonwinners under the original **mean**, not under every rule. HELM row 40 already needs zero median updates; retain that zero without attributing it to an intervention.\\n+\\n+### Reachability, not fictitious finite budgets\\n+\\n+At full headroom, all eight sampled targets per family are reachable under each rule. At half headroom, mean/median/Borda reachability counts are **5/5/4 for HELM** and **7/6/3 for Open LLM**, derived from the original final records. Keep the denominator at eight targets per family, not seeds times targets or controls plus originals.\\n+\\n+Report unreachable promotion as a status or infinity. Empty task lists accompanying unattainability are not zero-cost successes. Reference sentinels `d+1` are not investment values. Do not average away unattainable cases or compare finite-only averages without stating the changed population.\\n+\\n+### Allocation context and controls\\n+\\n+All original reference records in each family report exact bounds; feasible selected allocations match them. Explain the difference between an exact measured instance and a universally exact algorithm. A matched comparator example can show why immediate-gain greedy is not a median certificate: HELM row 28 at half headroom has median cost 21 versus the recorded greedy cost 52. Do not use this isolated comparison to claim general solver superiority.\\n+\\n+Selected original allocations repeat across the three recorded seeds. These are repeated static instances, not independently trained models or random population samples. Report repeatability and exact integers; do not invent confidence intervals, significance tests, or population uncertainty. Random-order baseline summaries may use only the recorded orders/seeds with their limited coverage disclosed.\\n+\\n+Permutation, constant-score, and zero-update cases are implementation controls. Keep them separate from empirical budget and reachability denominators. A synthetic constant table is not another public benchmark. Do not claim a completed replay result when its retained value is null.\\n+\\n+## Required figures and physical layout\\n+\\n+`paper.sty` sets a **single 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height**. Full text width and column width are therefore the same here; 0.48 text width is not a conference column. All three figures target width fraction 1.0. The generic historical style header does not authenticate the official ICLR 2026 template: verify official venue files before final drafting/submission.\\n+\\n+### Figure 1: score-space overview\\n+\\n+- **Generator:** `generate-concept-figure`, later orchestrator-owned; do not run it during strategy generation.\\n+- **Content:** public score table and frozen target/rivals, capped target-coordinate updates, the three rules, and minimum task count or unattainability. State that no training is performed. Use an equation and labels, not invented scores.\\n+- **Placement:** page 1 or 2, within the introduction/problem transition.\\n+- **Footprint:** approximately 5.5 by 2.3 inches plus caption, about 0.30 body page.\\n+- **Readability budget:** one panel, at most five major blocks and three rule branches; labels at least 9 pt. The arrows describe the mathematical mechanism, not research chronology.\\n+\\n+### Figure 2: paired mean/median budgets\\n+\\n+- **Generator:** `matplotlib`; four panels arranged 2 by 2, dataset by response cap.\\n+- **Axes:** exact/bounded minimum mean task fraction on x and median task fraction on y, both on `[0,1]`, with an equality diagonal.\\n+- **Data:** all eight original targets per panel; use no more than four status encodings. Eight points are observations, not eight colored series. Put unreachable cases in separate labeled marginal rails, with a corner category for both unreachable; never place a fabricated finite value on either numeric axis.\\n+- **Annotations:** highlight only the reversal exemplar in each dataset; show multiplicity for overlapping finite points. Do not turn repeated seeds into additional target points.\\n+- **Footprint:** approximately 5.5 by 3.8 inches plus caption, about 0.48 body page. At least 8.5-pt rendered ticks and 9-pt axis labels. Two panels per row, never four across.\\n+\\n+### Figure 3: cap-conditioned reachability\\n+\\n+- **Generator:** `matplotlib`; two panels, one per dataset.\\n+- **Axes:** three aggregation rules on x; reachable targets out of eight on a common 0-to-8 y axis. Two cap markers per rule and one shared cap legend.\\n+- **Footprint:** approximately 5.5 by 2.0 inches plus caption, about 0.26 body page; labels at least 9 pt.\\n+- **Meaning:** whether co-first is possible under the stated cap. Do not add another definition of success, optimizer scores, controls, or conditional finite-budget averages.\\n+\\n+All quantitative figures require source-generated **PDF and 300-DPI PNG**, with LaTeX referencing PDF when available. Keep editable source for the overview and obtain vector export when available. A raster-only full-width figure must be regenerated at **at least 2400 px wide**, never upscaled. Keep at most three panels per row and respect each figure'\\\"'s stricter panel/category/series budgets.\\n+\\n+## Body budget and scientific organization\\n+\\n+Target approximately **8.75 substantive body pages**, excluding references and supplementary information:\\n+\\n+| Component | Inclusive page budget |\\n+|---|---:|\\n+| Title and abstract | 0.50 |\\n+| Introduction, including early overview | 1.00 |\\n+| Related work | 0.50 |\\n+| Problem and intervention assumptions | 0.90 |\\n+| Allocation methods and certificate reasoning | 1.35 |\\n+| Public score tables and comparison protocol | 0.80 |\\n+| Results, including quantitative figures and compact tables | 2.45 |\\n+| Discussion and limitations | 0.90 |\\n+| Conclusion | 0.35 |\\n+\\n+The venue requires 8 to 9 body pages; if stopping at eight, page 8 must be at least 75 percent full. These numbers are layout allocations, not empirical findings. Spend space on why task-count support is an investment proxy, endpoint monotonicity, odd/even median mechanics, Borda rival updates, cap-dependent counterexamples, target-sampling asymmetry, and explicit failure conditions. Do not pad with search history, extra decorative plots, spacing tricks, or imagined experiments. If substantive compliance remains impossible, report insufficiency.\\n+\\n+Use thesis-first headings: **Problem and Intervention Model**, **Allocation Certificates**, **Public Score Tables and Comparison Protocol**, **Cap-Dependent Promotion Budgets**, and **Scope and Limitations**. Keep the core reversals, reachability failures, and noncausal limitations in the main body.\\n+\\n+## Supplementary information after references\\n+\\n+The supplement adds auditable depth rather than hiding evidence needed for the thesis. Its authoritative source IDs are listed in the JSON.\\n+\\n+1. **Endpoint reduction and scalar budget certificates.** Expand monotonicity and mean, odd-median, and even-median derivations using the selected source and recorded reference checks. No fabricated exhaustive tests or unverified theoretical novelty. Source: \\\"'`final.evidence.episodes`, with method files as supporting artifacts.\\n+2. **Rival-aware Borda constraints and bounded search.** Give pairwise credit definitions, changed rival totals, integer constraints, witness validation, deadline fallback, and observed reference-bound closure. No later refinements as ablations. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`, with the frozen source implementation.\\n+3. **Complete paired allocation and comparator tables.** Include every original target/rule/cap record, status, task count, normalized cost, bounds, and available greedy/random costs; retain seed-level differences where present. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`.\\n+4. **Matched controls and repeated-execution coverage.** Report control checks and retained failures separately from original data; distinguish repeatability from statistical population uncertainty. Do not fill missing replay evidence. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.*`.\\n+5. **Public snapshots, normalization, and reproduction map.** Document embedded upstream URLs/checksums, HELM v1.13.0 and pinned Open LLM contents revision, score scaling, acquisition dates, clustering/splitting, local hashes, and fixed target selection. Explain uncertain score-data redistribution rights and prefer upstream reacquisition. Map figures/tables to actual scripts, dependencies, commands, and evidence pointers when those scripts exist. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`, plus `task/resources/final/` metadata.\\n+6. **Selection scope and independent final assessment.** A short audit statement of tied search comparison and independent final outcome, not a candidate-by-candidate narrative. Sources: `study.selection_kind`, `study.investigation_outcome`, `study.search_score`, `study.baseline_score`, `study.search_score_delta`, `study.attempts.total/scored/unscored`, and `final.qualification.status/outcome/correct`.\\n+7. **LLM Usage.** Meet the venue requirement with precise documented roles in ideation, candidate-code assistance, query planning, framing, and later writing where actually used. Distinguish evaluator-produced results from assisted interpretation. Do not guess human effort, model identities, or unlogged verification. Sources: `study.selection_kind`, `final.evidence.episodes`, and available execution/source logs for role provenance.\\n+\\n+## Limitations and writing safeguards\\n+\\n+Essential main-text limitations are the hypothetical independent response cap; no calibrated training-cost interpretation; no transfer, forgetting, or reacting rivals; uniform task costs; co-first rather than strict victory; only two positive caps; normalization-dependent budgets; mean-based target sampling; finite public snapshots; and incomplete model-lineage knowledge. An unattainable result is impossibility **under the stated cap**, not a measured training loss or an invalid solver result.\\n+\\n+The final writer should build an extractor from `final.evidence.episodes`, check pairing and bounds, reproduce the displayed examples and counts, and separate originals from controls. Names of future scripts are plans, not existing deliverables. A clean reproduction map, paper figure/table scripts, authenticated venue files, and verified paper-level citations remain to be supplied. Do not claim measured CPU costs from episode duration or the original resource estimate.\\n+\\n+The bibliography is not yet a sufficient read-and-verified literature foundation. Many retrieved records are off-topic. `Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU` and `Brandt2016HandbookOC` have exact allowed-seed title matches, but metadata retrieval alone does not demonstrate content reading. Do not cite unrelated hits for padding, invent missing keys, or claim this stage read benchmark papers. Later source consultation must stay within the task'\\\"'s explicit exclusion boundary: exact allowed identifiers only, no citation-graph or related-content expansion, and stop/document accidental prohibited exposure. No external literature retrieval occurred in this framing stage. Do not assert first-ever novelty.\\n+\\n+Follow \\\"'`VENUE.md`: ICLR 2026, anonymous manuscript, no author identities, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, personal links, or internal absolute paths. Verify official template provenance and any actual venue checklist; do not import a checklist from another conference. Include `LLM Usage` after references.\\n+\\n+## Forbidden framing\\n+\\n+Avoid headings centered on **Search Progression**, **Campaign Phases**, **Milestones**, **Timeline**, **First Success**, **Experiment A / Experiment B**, **Cycle-by-Cycle Improvements**, **Winning Candidate**, and **Architecture Evolution**.\\n+\\n+Avoid phrases such as “we then tried,” “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “later variants,” “next-best,” and “search winner.” Do not claim “median is universally more robust,” “Borda is universally safer,” “measured training-data cost,” “proved contamination effectiveness,” “continuous cap frontier,” or state-of-the-art resistance.\\n+\\n+Internal labels banned from visible paper prose include `correctness rate`, `correct and eligible`, `eligible`, `combined_score`, `rubric`, `worker_01`, and `worker_02`. Their appearance in this memo is private audit guidance only. If a thresholded metric is used, give it one reader-facing meaning per figure; Figure 3 uses only reachability of co-first under the stated cap.\\n+\\n+## Stage completion criteria\\n+\\n+Validate the two strategy files for JSON shape, source-ID existence, consistent decisions, example/count grounding, figure budgets, and a nonempty grounded supplement. Do not draft `paper.tex` or `claims_grounding.json`, generate the figures, run new candidate experiments, or invoke final-paper validation in this stage.\\n*** End Patch\\nPATCH'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n50:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_31\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/sh -lc 'apply_patch() { codex --codex-run-as-apply-patch \\\"$(cat)\\\"; }\\napply_patch <<'\\\"'PATCH'\\n*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: paper_strategy.json\\n+{\\n+  \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ready\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"selected_prompt_family\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"paper_standalone\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"recommended_title\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"central_question\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"On fixed public multitask score tables, how does the smallest task subset needed to make a target co-first change with the aggregation rule and the allowed fraction of score headroom?\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"central_thesis\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"In the evaluated score tables, changing the response cap reverses mean-versus-median investment orderings for some targets and makes other promotions unattainable, so aggregation-rule resistance must be reported conditional on the cap and target score vector rather than as a universal ranking.\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"narrative_rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"This is a shared static score-space study with condition-specific evidence from HELM MMLU and the six-task Open LLM table, not two chronological experiments. Original final allocations contain independently bounded mean/median reversals in both settings, attainable and unattainable cases, and Borda comparisons. For HELM target row 10, mean/median costs are 9/12 tasks at full headroom and 23/12 at half headroom; for Open LLM row 1609 they are 2/3 and 4/3. These within-target comparisons support a precise conditional thesis without asserting attainable training responses or optimizer superiority. A broad solver-family story lacks ablations, a continuous-frontier story exceeds the two tested positive caps, and a search-improvement story contradicts the zero search delta. The frozen non-winning selection is still a defensible studied method: search selection and the independently completed final evaluation are different facts.\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"prior_paper_story_status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"rejected\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"edit_framing_decision\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Fresh framing, not an edit pass: no previous_paper manuscript, resolved edit-context files, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory was found. The required enum 'rejected' means no inherited manuscript story is adopted, not that an unseen paper was evaluated. Compare prospective cap frontiers, broad cross-setting solver improvements, and the measured cap-dependent budget story; select the last on direct evidence rather than retaining a memory-derived narrative.\\\\\\\",\\n+  \\\\\\\"staged_input_impact\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Direct inspection of source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py and METHOD.md establishes exact scalar allocation mechanisms, bounded rival-aware Borda search, hypothetical headroom responses, and a co-first criterion. These support a score-space budget study, not new training or architecture claims.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The selected staged program source has the same file SHA-256 as ../downstream/program/src/program.py. Selection metadata directly identifies the first recorded candidate; later gain-window and mixture-cut candidates are not the studied method. Different recorded directory-tree hashes belong to different artifact scopes and are not silently equated.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py supplies the original comparator implementation. Its matched search score is retained as a limited selection fact, not replaced by an invented final baseline result.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Final original-case records, read independently of downstream_study, change the emphasis from prospective frontiers to two measured positive cap levels, exact reference budgets, and cap-conditioned reversals. Permutation, constant-score, and zero-update cases are controls rather than extra datasets.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The available ICLR style is single-column with a 5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height. Its generic historical header does not authenticate an official 2026 release; the writer must verify the official venue files before final submission.\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"method_groups\\\\\\\": [\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Static capped-response promotion model: mean, ordinary median, and Borda\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"supporting\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Defines the common scientific object: choose a target-only task subset, set selected scores to x + rho(1-x), keep rival score coordinates fixed, and minimize subset size subject to co-first tolerance. The three rules expose distinct score and rank mechanisms under the same intervention.\\\\\\\"\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Frozen allocation solver: exact scalar budgets and bounded rival-aware Borda search\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"proposed\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"This is the selected configuration actually linked to final evidence. Explain sorted gains for mean, threshold counting for odd median, endpoint counting for even median, and integer Borda gaps with a verified feasible fallback. Proposed means studied configuration, not demonstrated superiority or a novelty claim.\\\\\\\"\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Original frozen comparator\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"baseline\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Retain the original baseline where evidence exists: study.baseline_score and study.search_score_delta establish a tied search comparison. There is no separately grounded final evaluation of that full comparator, so do not extend the comparison to final results.\\\\\\\"\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Evaluator-recorded no-update, random-order, and immediate-gain greedy policies\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"baseline\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The final episode records contain matched task witnesses and bounds for these policies. Use no-update to expose already-co-first targets under alternative rules and use greedy/random for bounded descriptive allocation comparisons. Do not assume the evaluator's greedy policy is the original frozen baseline without an explicit implementation match.\\\\\\\"\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Independent exact or bounded reference allocation\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"supporting\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"include_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Reference lower/upper bounds and independently checked witnesses establish what is minimal in each reported decision. In original cases the reference exact flags are true and feasible selected cardinalities match their bounds; this supports exactness for those observations, not a universal exact Borda algorithm.\\\\\\\"\\n+    }\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"results_focus\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Primary comparison: paired mean and ordinary-median minimum task budgets for the same target at rho=1 and rho=0.5, separately for HELM MMLU and Open LLM. Ground every point in final.evidence.episodes, filtering solution.cases to case=original and pairing by family, target, rule, fraction, and seed.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Show both measured reversals explicitly: HELM row 10 changes from mean/median 9/12 to 23/12 tasks; Open LLM row 1609 changes from 2/3 to 4/3. These are descriptive counterexamples to a cap-independent ordering, not estimates of how frequently reversal occurs in future populations.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Report all sampled targets, including zero-cost cases and unattainable cases. Target selection is by original mean nonwinner rank, not by being a nonwinner under every rule: HELM row 40 already needs zero median updates. Do not discard it or reinterpret zero investment as an intervention effect.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Reachability under a fixed cap across all three rules: at full headroom all eight evaluated targets per family are reachable; at half headroom HELM mean/median/Borda counts are 5/5/4 and Open LLM counts are 7/6/3. The fixed denominator is eight original targets per family, not repeated seeds or controls. Use final.evidence.episodes; never count an unattainable empty witness as zero cost.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Explain solver mechanism without a superiority narrative: on the evaluated original decisions, feasible selected cardinalities match independently exact reference bounds. Matched greedy excess-task examples can illustrate threshold plateaus, such as HELM row 28 at half cap, where median needs 21 tasks versus the recorded greedy 52. Random policies have only the recorded seed/order coverage; no broad statistical claim follows.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Use matched permutation, constant-score, and zero-update controls as implementation checks only. Keep their contributions out of the scientific budget and reachability denominators; synthetic constant tables are not public empirical evidence.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Keep selection and final evidence separate: study.* records a non-winning selection, three scored attempts, zero unscored attempts, and zero search-score difference from the original baseline. final.qualification.* independently reports completed and qualified. These facts neither establish solver improvement nor establish causal training efficacy, universal validity, or unseen-family generalization.\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"figure_plan\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Figure 1, early overview: public score table plus frozen target and rivals -> capped target-coordinate updates -> mean/ordinary median/Borda -> minimum selected-task count or unattainability. Make the counterfactual assumption and absence of model training instantly visible.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Figure 2, main paired comparison: a 2-by-2 scatter matrix, dataset by positive cap, with mean minimum task fraction on x, median minimum task fraction on y, and an equality diagonal. Plot all eight targets in each panel; handle unattainability in explicitly labeled marginal rails, not as a numeric cost. Highlight only the preidentified within-target reversal examples.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Figure 3, reachability comparison: two panels, one per dataset, with the three aggregation rules on x and the count of reachable targets out of eight on y, comparing full and half headroom. This brings Borda and lost attainability into the main paper without conflating them with finite-budget averages.\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"figure_specs\\\\\\\": [\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"score_space_overview\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"required\\\\\\\": true,\\n+      \\\\\\\"purpose\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Give a page-1-or-2 visual definition of the score-space intervention, the common comparison design, and the distinction between a finite minimum and unattainability; use equations and labels, not fabricated example scores.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"diagram\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_generator\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"generate-concept-figure\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"group_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"scientific_object\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"metric\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"minimum_selected_task_count_or_unattainability\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"split_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"aggregation_rule\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_span\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"full_width\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"target_width_fraction\\\\\\\": 1.0,\\n+      \\\\\\\"layout_rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"paper.sty sets a single 5.5-inch text column, so full width equals column width; there is no two-column spanning environment to exploit. Use one horizontal conceptual panel, at most five major blocks and three rule branches, about 5.5 by 2.3 inches plus caption, occupying about 0.30 body page. Render all labels at least 9 pt. A half-width diagram would make the intervention and noncausal caveat illegible.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"allow_progression\\\\\\\": false,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_panels\\\\\\\": 1,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\\\\\": 5,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_series_per_panel\\\\\\\": 3\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"paired_mean_median_budgets\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"required\\\\\\\": true,\\n+      \\\\\\\"purpose\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Expose the within-target cap-dependent change in mean/median budget ordering across the two evaluated score tables, retaining all finite and unattainable observations.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"paired_scatter\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_generator\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"matplotlib\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"group_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"response_cap\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"metric\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"independently_bounded_minimum_selected_task_fraction\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"split_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"dataset_and_response_cap\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_span\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"full_width\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"target_width_fraction\\\\\\\": 1.0,\\n+      \\\\\\\"layout_rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"At the inspected 5.5-inch single-column width, use four panels in two rows, never four across: about 5.5 by 3.8 inches plus caption, about 0.48 body page. Keep both numeric axes on [0,1], place unreachable states in separated labeled rails, and use at least 8.5-pt rendered ticks and 9-pt axis labels. Eight target points are observations, not eight colored categories or legend series. Use no more than four status encodings, annotate only the reversal exemplar in each dataset, and show multiplicity when finite points overlap. Repeated seeds must not be jittered into apparent independent target observations.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"allow_progression\\\\\\\": false,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_panels\\\\\\\": 4,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\\\\\": 4,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_series_per_panel\\\\\\\": 4\\n+    },\\n+    {\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"cap_conditioned_reachability\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"required\\\\\\\": true,\\n+      \\\\\\\"purpose\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare whether promotion is possible under each rule and cap, including Borda, using the same fixed target denominator in every panel.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"figure_type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"grouped_dot\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_generator\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"matplotlib\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"group_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"aggregation_rule\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"metric\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"reachable_target_count_out_of_eight\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"split_by\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"dataset\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"preferred_span\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"full_width\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"target_width_fraction\\\\\\\": 1.0,\\n+      \\\\\\\"layout_rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Use two side-by-side panels within the single 5.5-inch text column, three rule labels and two cap markers per panel, about 5.5 by 2.0 inches plus caption or 0.26 body page. Keep a common 0-to-8 count axis and one shared cap legend at 9 pt or larger. Do not add budget averages, optimizer scores, controls, or a second definition of success to this figure.\\\\\\\",\\n+      \\\\\\\"allow_progression\\\\\\\": false,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_panels\\\\\\\": 2,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\\\\\": 3,\\n+      \\\\\\\"max_series_per_panel\\\\\\\": 2\\n+    }\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"supplementary_plan\\\\\\\": {\\n+    \\\\\\\"purpose\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"After references, provide the exact derivations, complete paired allocation evidence, control results, and reproducibility/provenance details needed to audit the static study, without moving the central reversals, unattainability, or noncausal limitations out of the main body.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"planned_subsections\\\\\\\": [\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Endpoint reduction and scalar budget certificates\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Expand the monotonicity argument for endpoint subsets; derive the mean sorted-gain minimum, the odd-median threshold count, and the even-median two-order-statistic endpoint procedure. Use the selected program and METHOD.md as implementation evidence and the reference lower/upper fields in final episodes as measured checks. Separate code-level derivations from measured findings; do not invent exhaustive-test results or claim theoretical novelty from source inspection alone.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Rival-aware Borda constraints and bounded search\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document pairwise win credits with half-credit ties, target-induced rival aggregate changes, integer gap constraints, incumbent validation, and deadline fallback from the frozen selected source. Tabulate recorded independent reference lower/upper bounds, including whether they close; do not import later gain-window or mixture-cut implementations as evaluated ablations.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Complete paired allocation and comparator tables\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"List every original target/rule/cap record, task count, normalized cost, status, reference bounds, and available greedy/random witness costs, with seed-level records where policies differ. Retain already-co-first and unattainable cases. Derive all tables directly from final.evidence.episodes and preserve explicit episode/case/observation pointers. No d+1 sentinel may be printed as an investment.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Matched controls and repeated-execution coverage\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report permutation, constant-score, and zero-update cases separately from original tables, using recorded validation flags and failures. Explain that three seeds repeat the same fixed target instances; identical selected allocations indicate repeatability, not uncertainty about a model population. Replay is only present if actually recorded and has no statistical weight; do not invent a replay result when solution.replay is null.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.status\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.correct\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Public snapshots, normalization, and reproduction map\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document task/resources/final/helm.json and openllm.json plus protocol.json, source URLs and checksums embedded in row provenance, HELM release v1.13.0, the pinned Open LLM contents revision, score scaling, fixed target selection, hash/alias clustering, and acquisition dates. Distinguish local resource hashes, upstream byte hashes, and transfer hashes. Record source-data license ambiguity and reacquisition rather than assuming model/code licenses cover score redistribution. Map each planned figure/table to an extraction script, environment, command, and actual evidence pointers when implemented; script names in this strategy are plans, not existing deliverables.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Selection scope and independent final assessment\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"A short audit statement, not a search narrative: selection used three scored attempts and zero unscored attempts with no search-score improvement over the original baseline. Record the independent completed/qualified final outcome using final.qualification.*, not inferred from selection. Keep search and final weighted scores in separate scopes; there is no matched final original-baseline delta. Do not turn an absent diagnostic into a passed gate or infer a failure mechanism that an evaluator did not identify.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"study.selection_kind\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.investigation_outcome\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.search_score\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.baseline_score\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.search_score_delta\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.attempts.total\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.attempts.scored\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"study.attempts.unscored\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.status\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.qualification.correct\\\\\\\"]\\n+      },\\n+      {\\n+        \\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"LLM Usage\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"grounded_content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Disclose the roles evidenced by the supplied source/selection artifacts and this stage's recorded logs: research-idea assistance, candidate-code assistance where documented, reference-query planning, paper framing, and subsequent writing assistance if actually used. State that quantitative evidence is evaluator-produced and that no training intervention was performed. Verify precise human/LLM roles against available records rather than guessing model identities, manual effort, or validation activity.\\\\\\\",\\n+        \\\\\\\"source_ids\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"study.selection_kind\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\\\\\"]\\n+      }\\n+    ]\\n+  },\\n+  \\\\\\\"forbidden_headings\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Search Progression\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Campaign Phases\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Milestones\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Timeline\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"First Success\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Experiment A / Experiment B\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Cycle-by-Cycle Improvements\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Winning Candidate\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Architecture Evolution\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"forbidden_phrases\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"we then tried\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"first valid result\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"subsequent improvements\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"later variants\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"next-best\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"search winner\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"state-of-the-art manipulation resistance\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"median is universally more robust\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Borda is universally safer\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"measured training-data cost\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"proved contamination effectiveness\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"continuous cap frontier\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"correctness rate\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"correct and eligible\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"eligible\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"combined_score\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"rubric\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"worker_01\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"worker_02\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"notes_for_writer\\\\\\\": [\\n+    \\\\\\\"Scope of this stage: strategy only. Do not create paper.tex or claims_grounding.json, invoke final-paper validation, generate figures, or run new candidate experiments in this stage. The paper_standalone family calls for thesis-first organization, not discovery order.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Body organization and inclusive page allocation: title/abstract 0.50 page; introduction including early overview 1.00; related work 0.50; problem and intervention assumptions 0.90; allocation methods and certificate reasoning 1.35; data and comparison protocol 0.80; results including both quantitative figures and compact evidence tables 2.45; discussion and limitations 0.90; conclusion 0.35. Total target is 8.75 body pages, excluding references and substantive supplementary material. The venue accepts only 8 to 9 body pages, with page 8 at least 75 percent full if the body stops there. These are layout budgets, not empirical measurements. Do not use spacing tricks or invented experiments to reach them.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Use the page budget for scientific depth: define why task-count support is an investment proxy; derive endpoint reduction and median plateaus; distinguish odd/even medians and updated-rival Borda; show an exact cap-reversal table with separate rows for the two caps; discuss finite costs versus impossibility and the mean-based target sampling asymmetry. Keep figures near the explanations they support. If the available material still cannot fill a substantive compliant body, report insufficiency rather than add filler.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Use reader-facing section names such as Problem and Intervention Model, Allocation Certificates, Public Score Tables and Comparison Protocol, Cap-Dependent Promotion Budgets, and Scope and Limitations. The main scientific comparisons are aggregation rules under common interventions; solver baselines establish allocation context and are not the thesis.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Define x in [0,1], selected subset S, c_j(rho)=round(x_j+rho(1-x_j),10), uniform investment |S|, and normalized investment |S|/d. Co-first permits an aggregate deficit at most 1e-10; it is not strict first place. Ordinary even median averages its two middle scores. Borda is average pairwise win credit with half credit for ties, and rivals' aggregates must be recomputed after target improvement. Fixed rival coordinates do not imply fixed Borda totals.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Use only the two evaluated positive response fractions, 1 and 0.5, for the empirical cap comparison. The zero fraction belongs to the no-update control. Endpoint monotonicity supplies a model-level argument, not evidence for continuous response curves, strict-margin sensitivity, empirical-cohort ceilings, composition resampling, or actual training effects. Those experiments are absent.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Evidence mapping: use final.evidence.episodes for every main numerical allocation, count, example, and quantitative figure; the payload is paper_data.json.final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes. Use its exact episode/case/observation locations in downstream claim mapping. Method derivations additionally cite source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py and METHOD.md; snapshot context additionally uses task/resources/final/{helm,openllm,protocol}.json. These file paths are supporting artifacts, not newly invented grounding-source IDs.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Private-only audit context: study.selection_kind is non_winner_candidate; study.investigation_outcome is completed_no_winner; study.search_score equals study.baseline_score at 0.7239766081871346; study.search_score_delta is 0; study.attempts.total/scored/unscored are 3/3/0. final.qualification.status/outcome/correct are completed/qualified/true. final.metric.final_score is 0.6633040935672518 in a separate weighted evaluation scope. Do not subtract final and search scores or expose their internal field labels as scientific metrics. No failed final qualification is reported in this run; do not invent one or turn search non-selection into a validity failure. Missing gate-level diagnostics remain unknown.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The final evidence source marks candidate diagnostics unverified. Rely on independently recorded validation checks and reference lower/upper bounds, not candidate self-reports. Exact Borda costs can be asserted for recorded decisions with matching feasible witness size and exact reference bounds; the selected Borda algorithm itself remains deadline-bounded. Qualification is not a guarantee of novelty, causal validity, generalization, or submission completeness.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The final resource context is 57 HELM subjects and six normalized Open LLM task columns; final populations contain 42 and 2129 rows respectively. Use frozen resource metadata to document these context facts, not the task brief's broader public-catalog totals. Do not treat the six aggregate columns as six collections of all their subtask scores, and do not include Raw columns as independent tasks. Target labels in observations are local row indices, not public model identifiers; recover display names from the matching frozen resource only when needed.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Uncertainty: retain seed-level results, but distinguish execution seeds from training runs or random population samples. Selected original allocations are identical across the three recorded seeds. Report that fact and exact integer budgets rather than fabricated confidence intervals or significance tests. Random-order comparator variability can be summarized only from actual recorded orders/seeds with its small coverage stated. The eight rank-stratified mean-nonwinner targets per family are not a random sample of all models. Do not promise bootstrap, unseen-family, or composition robustness analyses absent from the artifacts.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Essential limitations belong in the abstract/method and main discussion: assumed independent capped task responses, no measured tokens/data/dollars, no transfer or forgetting, stationary rival score vectors, uniform rather than task-specific cost, co-first rather than strict win, only two positive caps, normalization-dependent comparisons, target-selection asymmetry, and finite snapshot coverage. An unattainable observation means failure under the stated score cap, not a measured training loss or algorithmic invalidity.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Planned quantitative implementation: write a downstream extractor for final.evidence.episodes that emits tidy original records and separately marked controls, verifies pairing and reference bounds, and reproduces the stated examples/counts before plotting. Collapse identical selected records across seeds only with an explicit repeatability note. Use infinity/status for impossible promotion; never use the reference d+1 sentinel as a finite budget, never average infinities away, and never score an empty unattainable allocation as zero investment.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Figure export contract: quantitative plots must be source-generated as PDF plus a 300-DPI PNG, and LaTeX must use the PDF when available. Every figure targets the inspected full 5.5-inch text column; do not treat 0.48 text width as a conference column. Use no more than three panels per row and the explicit per-figure label/series budgets. If only raster is available, regenerate from source at at least 2400 px width for these full-width figures; never upscale an existing raster.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The single required overview uses generate-concept-figure through the later orchestrator, not an agent invocation in this stage. Keep editable diagram source; obtain a vector PDF from its native diagram export when available. If only raster export is available, enforce the full-width raster floor. The overview should depict a score table and a hypothetical intervention, not a neural architecture, training pipeline, discovery timeline, or fabricated benchmark values.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"The supplied bibliography is not yet a sufficient read-and-verified literature basis. Inspection found many off-topic query matches; do not cite them to fill a related-work section. Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU and Brandt2016HandbookOC have exact task-allowed title matches in references_provenance.json, but their retrieved metadata does not demonstrate full-text reading. Any later use must verify and actually consult permitted source material. Missing benchmark-context references require safe exact-identifier retrieval and provenance before citation; do not invent citation keys or pretend this stage read those papers.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Respect the excluded-literature boundary: do not identify or search for the prohibited prior study, expand citation graphs, browse related/citing material, or infer its results. No external literature retrieval was performed in this framing stage. Future verification should use only explicitly task-allowed identifiers and stop/document any accidental prohibited exposure. Position the contribution as a reproducible conditional analysis, not a first-ever result unsupported by a safe literature review.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Apply VENUE.md: ICLR 2026, double-blind, no author identities, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, personal links, or internal absolute paths in the manuscript. Verify official template provenance separately from the observed geometry. Include the grounded LLM Usage section after references, and satisfy any verified current venue-required checklist rather than inventing a NeurIPS checklist for ICLR.\\\\\\\",\\n+    \\\\\\\"Reproducibility is a remaining deliverable, not an accomplished claim: selected solver source and frozen input/evaluator artifacts exist, but paper figure/table scripts, a clean reader-facing reproduction command map, verified paper-level citations, and authenticated venue files are not yet supplied by this strategy. Reuse recorded source URLs, acquisition dates, seeds, and byte checksums; do not invent measured CPU time from resource estimates or episode durations. Preserve the original baseline comparison where supported, and explicitly omit any unmatched final full-program baseline comparison.\\\\\\\"\\n+  ],\\n+  \\\\\\\"failure_reason\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\n+}\\n*** Add File: paper_strategy.md\\n+# Paper strategy: cap-dependent task budgets\\n+\\n+**Status:** ready for the \\\"'`paper_standalone` family. This is a framing plan, not a manuscript or a statement that final submission requirements have already been met.\\n+\\n+**Provisional title:** Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion.\\n+\\n+## Central question and thesis\\n+\\n+**Question.** On fixed public multitask score tables, how does the smallest task subset needed to make a target co-first change with the aggregation rule and the allowed fraction of score headroom?\\n+\\n+**Thesis.** In the evaluated score tables, changing the response cap reverses mean-versus-median investment orderings for some targets and makes other promotions unattainable. Aggregation-rule resistance must therefore be reported conditional on the cap and target score vector, rather than as a universal ranking.\\n+\\n+Present HELM MMLU and the six-task Open LLM table as two settings of one static intervention model. Do not organize them as chronological experiments. The contribution is a conditional, reproducible score-space analysis with explicit allocation certificates and failure cases, not a training method, an architecture, or a demonstrated optimizer improvement.\\n+\\n+## Framing decision and alternatives\\n+\\n+This is not an edit pass. No seeded prior manuscript, resolved edit-context files, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` were found. The JSON enum `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` means no inherited manuscript story is adopted; it does not mean an unseen manuscript was assessed.\\n+\\n+The selected source and original comparator were inspected directly. The selected staged `program.py` matches the downstream source file by SHA-256. Selection metadata identifies the first candidate, not either later refinement. Recorded directory-tree hashes differ across artifact scopes; do not equate them based on a matching source file.\\n+\\n+Three candidate stories were considered:\\n+\\n+- **Prospective cap frontiers:** preserve the earlier conceptual direction, but narrow its language. Only two positive caps were evaluated; continuous frontiers, empirical response envelopes, strict-margin sensitivity, and composition resampling are not completed evidence.\\n+- **Broad cross-setting solver improvements:** reject as the center. There are no grounded ablations attributing gains to the later gain-window or mixture-cut variants, and search scores tie the original comparator.\\n+- **Measured cap-dependent promotion budgets:** select. The same-target rule comparisons directly demonstrate reversals in both score tables, while reachability and independent bounds make the limitations inspectable.\\n+\\n+This choice beats a campaign diary because it answers the original scientific question with controlled, interpretable comparisons. Neither a positive search winner nor model training is needed for this bounded study.\\n+\\n+## Independent final assessment and evidence hierarchy\\n+\\n+Read final assessment independently of selection. The following are **private audit facts**, not names for visible scientific metrics:\\n+\\n+- `study.selection_kind = non_winner_candidate`; `study.investigation_outcome = completed_no_winner`.\\n+- `study.search_score = study.baseline_score = 0.7239766081871346`; `study.search_score_delta = 0`.\\n+- `study.attempts.total/scored/unscored = 3/3/0`. Unscored attempts would be missing performance evidence, not measured losses.\\n+- `final.qualification.status/outcome/correct = completed/qualified/true`.\\n+- `final.metric.final_score = 0.6633040935672518` belongs to a separate weighted evaluation scope. It is not a matched change from the search score.\\n+\\n+No failed final qualification is reported in this run. Do not invent one, reinterpret non-selection as a validity failure, or treat passing assessment as proof of generalization or scientific novelty. Missing criterion diagnostics remain unknown. Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified; use recorded independent validation and reference bounds instead of self-report.\\n+\\n+Main quantitative grounding is `final.evidence.episodes`, whose payload is `paper_data.json.final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes`. Filter `solution.cases` to `case = original`, then pair by family, target, rule, fraction, and seed. Retain exact episode/case/observation pointers for downstream claim mapping. The method files and `task/resources/final/` files support implementation and provenance explanations; their paths are not invented grounding IDs.\\n+\\n+## Scientific definitions and scope\\n+\\n+For normalized target scores `x` and a selected task subset `S`, selecting coordinate `j` changes it to `round(x_j + rho(1-x_j), 10)`. Other target coordinates and every rival score coordinate remain fixed. Investment is the uniform task count `|S|`, optionally divided by task count `d` for cross-setting display. The empirical positive caps are `rho = 1` and `rho = 0.5`; zero belongs to the no-update control.\\n+\\n+Promotion means **co-first** within aggregate tolerance `1e-10`, not strict first place. The ordinary even median averages its two central values. Borda uses average pairwise win credit with half credit for ties: changing the target can change rivals'\\\"' Borda totals despite fixed rival task scores. Recompute those totals.\\n+\\n+This is a hypothetical score-response model. It does not measure training examples, tokens, money, compute, contamination efficacy, transfer, forgetting, or rivals' strategic responses. Only under a genuine upper-response envelope could an exact support minimum lower-bound affected tasks for a successful intervention; no evidence here calibrates such an envelope to real training.\\n+\\n+The frozen final context contains 57 HELM subjects and six normalized Open LLM columns, with 42 and 2129 rows respectively. These are resource-context facts, not the broader public-catalog totals from the task brief. Do not mix Raw columns into the six-task analysis or reinterpret their aggregate columns as all underlying subtasks. Target row numbers are local identifiers, not model names.\\n+\\n+## Narrow method groups\\n+\\n+1. **Static capped-response model, supporting:** the shared intervention and the three aggregation rules define the comparison.\\n+2. **Frozen allocation solver, studied/proposed:** sorted gains for mean, threshold counting for odd median, endpoint counting for even median, and bounded rival-aware integer Borda search with verified fallback. The label proposed does not assert novelty or superiority.\\n+3. **Original frozen comparator, baseline:** preserve its tied search comparison using \\\"'`study.baseline_score` and `study.search_score_delta`. No separate matched final evaluation of that full implementation is grounded.\\n+4. **Evaluator-recorded policies, baselines:** no update, random task order, and immediate-gain greedy. These have matched final allocation records; do not silently relabel the evaluator'\\\"'s greedy policy as the original frozen comparator.\\n+5. **Independent budget reference, supporting:** exact flags and lower/upper bounds certify individual reported costs. Finite selected allocations match exact reference cardinalities in the original records. This is not a proof that the deadline-bounded Borda algorithm is exact on every input.\\n+\\n+Exclude later gain-window and mixture-cut branches from the method narrative. They are not ablations, and their names are not evidence of an improved mechanism. Do not introduce unmeasured architecture shifts or training procedures.\\n+\\n+## Results that carry the argument\\n+\\n+### Cap-conditioned budget ordering\\n+\\n+Put this small table in the main body; each entry is a task count from \\\"'`final.evidence.episodes`, not a training cost:\\n+\\n+| Score table | Local target row | Headroom fraction | Mean | Median | Borda |\\n+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n+| HELM | 10 | 1 | 9 | 12 | 34 |\\n+| HELM | 10 | 0.5 | 23 | 12 | 39 |\\n+| Open LLM | 1609 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 5 |\\n+| Open LLM | 1609 | 0.5 | 4 | 3 | 5 |\\n+\\n+The within-target mean/median ordering reverses in each setting. These are counterexamples to an unconditional ordering, not estimates of reversal frequency in future model populations. Explain them using cumulative score gains versus order-statistic thresholds, not an untested causal account of developer behavior.\\n+\\n+Show all sampled targets in the main comparison, not only these examples. Targets were selected as nonwinners under the original **mean**, not under every rule. HELM row 40 already needs zero median updates; retain that zero without attributing it to an intervention.\\n+\\n+### Reachability, not fictitious finite budgets\\n+\\n+At full headroom, all eight sampled targets per family are reachable under each rule. At half headroom, mean/median/Borda reachability counts are **5/5/4 for HELM** and **7/6/3 for Open LLM**, derived from the original final records. Keep the denominator at eight targets per family, not seeds times targets or controls plus originals.\\n+\\n+Report unreachable promotion as a status or infinity. Empty task lists accompanying unattainability are not zero-cost successes. Reference sentinels `d+1` are not investment values. Do not average away unattainable cases or compare finite-only averages without stating the changed population.\\n+\\n+### Allocation context and controls\\n+\\n+All original reference records in each family report exact bounds; feasible selected allocations match them. Explain the difference between an exact measured instance and a universally exact algorithm. A matched comparator example can show why immediate-gain greedy is not a median certificate: HELM row 28 at half headroom has median cost 21 versus the recorded greedy cost 52. Do not use this isolated comparison to claim general solver superiority.\\n+\\n+Selected original allocations repeat across the three recorded seeds. These are repeated static instances, not independently trained models or random population samples. Report repeatability and exact integers; do not invent confidence intervals, significance tests, or population uncertainty. Random-order baseline summaries may use only the recorded orders/seeds with their limited coverage disclosed.\\n+\\n+Permutation, constant-score, and zero-update cases are implementation controls. Keep them separate from empirical budget and reachability denominators. A synthetic constant table is not another public benchmark. Do not claim a completed replay result when its retained value is null.\\n+\\n+## Required figures and physical layout\\n+\\n+`paper.sty` sets a **single 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height**. Full text width and column width are therefore the same here; 0.48 text width is not a conference column. All three figures target width fraction 1.0. The generic historical style header does not authenticate the official ICLR 2026 template: verify official venue files before final drafting/submission.\\n+\\n+### Figure 1: score-space overview\\n+\\n+- **Generator:** `generate-concept-figure`, later orchestrator-owned; do not run it during strategy generation.\\n+- **Content:** public score table and frozen target/rivals, capped target-coordinate updates, the three rules, and minimum task count or unattainability. State that no training is performed. Use an equation and labels, not invented scores.\\n+- **Placement:** page 1 or 2, within the introduction/problem transition.\\n+- **Footprint:** approximately 5.5 by 2.3 inches plus caption, about 0.30 body page.\\n+- **Readability budget:** one panel, at most five major blocks and three rule branches; labels at least 9 pt. The arrows describe the mathematical mechanism, not research chronology.\\n+\\n+### Figure 2: paired mean/median budgets\\n+\\n+- **Generator:** `matplotlib`; four panels arranged 2 by 2, dataset by response cap.\\n+- **Axes:** exact/bounded minimum mean task fraction on x and median task fraction on y, both on `[0,1]`, with an equality diagonal.\\n+- **Data:** all eight original targets per panel; use no more than four status encodings. Eight points are observations, not eight colored series. Put unreachable cases in separate labeled marginal rails, with a corner category for both unreachable; never place a fabricated finite value on either numeric axis.\\n+- **Annotations:** highlight only the reversal exemplar in each dataset; show multiplicity for overlapping finite points. Do not turn repeated seeds into additional target points.\\n+- **Footprint:** approximately 5.5 by 3.8 inches plus caption, about 0.48 body page. At least 8.5-pt rendered ticks and 9-pt axis labels. Two panels per row, never four across.\\n+\\n+### Figure 3: cap-conditioned reachability\\n+\\n+- **Generator:** `matplotlib`; two panels, one per dataset.\\n+- **Axes:** three aggregation rules on x; reachable targets out of eight on a common 0-to-8 y axis. Two cap markers per rule and one shared cap legend.\\n+- **Footprint:** approximately 5.5 by 2.0 inches plus caption, about 0.26 body page; labels at least 9 pt.\\n+- **Meaning:** whether co-first is possible under the stated cap. Do not add another definition of success, optimizer scores, controls, or conditional finite-budget averages.\\n+\\n+All quantitative figures require source-generated **PDF and 300-DPI PNG**, with LaTeX referencing PDF when available. Keep editable source for the overview and obtain vector export when available. A raster-only full-width figure must be regenerated at **at least 2400 px wide**, never upscaled. Keep at most three panels per row and respect each figure'\\\"'s stricter panel/category/series budgets.\\n+\\n+## Body budget and scientific organization\\n+\\n+Target approximately **8.75 substantive body pages**, excluding references and supplementary information:\\n+\\n+| Component | Inclusive page budget |\\n+|---|---:|\\n+| Title and abstract | 0.50 |\\n+| Introduction, including early overview | 1.00 |\\n+| Related work | 0.50 |\\n+| Problem and intervention assumptions | 0.90 |\\n+| Allocation methods and certificate reasoning | 1.35 |\\n+| Public score tables and comparison protocol | 0.80 |\\n+| Results, including quantitative figures and compact tables | 2.45 |\\n+| Discussion and limitations | 0.90 |\\n+| Conclusion | 0.35 |\\n+\\n+The venue requires 8 to 9 body pages; if stopping at eight, page 8 must be at least 75 percent full. These numbers are layout allocations, not empirical findings. Spend space on why task-count support is an investment proxy, endpoint monotonicity, odd/even median mechanics, Borda rival updates, cap-dependent counterexamples, target-sampling asymmetry, and explicit failure conditions. Do not pad with search history, extra decorative plots, spacing tricks, or imagined experiments. If substantive compliance remains impossible, report insufficiency.\\n+\\n+Use thesis-first headings: **Problem and Intervention Model**, **Allocation Certificates**, **Public Score Tables and Comparison Protocol**, **Cap-Dependent Promotion Budgets**, and **Scope and Limitations**. Keep the core reversals, reachability failures, and noncausal limitations in the main body.\\n+\\n+## Supplementary information after references\\n+\\n+The supplement adds auditable depth rather than hiding evidence needed for the thesis. Its authoritative source IDs are listed in the JSON.\\n+\\n+1. **Endpoint reduction and scalar budget certificates.** Expand monotonicity and mean, odd-median, and even-median derivations using the selected source and recorded reference checks. No fabricated exhaustive tests or unverified theoretical novelty. Source: \\\"'`final.evidence.episodes`, with method files as supporting artifacts.\\n+2. **Rival-aware Borda constraints and bounded search.** Give pairwise credit definitions, changed rival totals, integer constraints, witness validation, deadline fallback, and observed reference-bound closure. No later refinements as ablations. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`, with the frozen source implementation.\\n+3. **Complete paired allocation and comparator tables.** Include every original target/rule/cap record, status, task count, normalized cost, bounds, and available greedy/random costs; retain seed-level differences where present. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`.\\n+4. **Matched controls and repeated-execution coverage.** Report control checks and retained failures separately from original data; distinguish repeatability from statistical population uncertainty. Do not fill missing replay evidence. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.*`.\\n+5. **Public snapshots, normalization, and reproduction map.** Document embedded upstream URLs/checksums, HELM v1.13.0 and pinned Open LLM contents revision, score scaling, acquisition dates, clustering/splitting, local hashes, and fixed target selection. Explain uncertain score-data redistribution rights and prefer upstream reacquisition. Map figures/tables to actual scripts, dependencies, commands, and evidence pointers when those scripts exist. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`, plus `task/resources/final/` metadata.\\n+6. **Selection scope and independent final assessment.** A short audit statement of tied search comparison and independent final outcome, not a candidate-by-candidate narrative. Sources: `study.selection_kind`, `study.investigation_outcome`, `study.search_score`, `study.baseline_score`, `study.search_score_delta`, `study.attempts.total/scored/unscored`, and `final.qualification.status/outcome/correct`.\\n+7. **LLM Usage.** Meet the venue requirement with precise documented roles in ideation, candidate-code assistance, query planning, framing, and later writing where actually used. Distinguish evaluator-produced results from assisted interpretation. Do not guess human effort, model identities, or unlogged verification. Sources: `study.selection_kind`, `final.evidence.episodes`, and available execution/source logs for role provenance.\\n+\\n+## Limitations and writing safeguards\\n+\\n+Essential main-text limitations are the hypothetical independent response cap; no calibrated training-cost interpretation; no transfer, forgetting, or reacting rivals; uniform task costs; co-first rather than strict victory; only two positive caps; normalization-dependent budgets; mean-based target sampling; finite public snapshots; and incomplete model-lineage knowledge. An unattainable result is impossibility **under the stated cap**, not a measured training loss or an invalid solver result.\\n+\\n+The final writer should build an extractor from `final.evidence.episodes`, check pairing and bounds, reproduce the displayed examples and counts, and separate originals from controls. Names of future scripts are plans, not existing deliverables. A clean reproduction map, paper figure/table scripts, authenticated venue files, and verified paper-level citations remain to be supplied. Do not claim measured CPU costs from episode duration or the original resource estimate.\\n+\\n+The bibliography is not yet a sufficient read-and-verified literature foundation. Many retrieved records are off-topic. `Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU` and `Brandt2016HandbookOC` have exact allowed-seed title matches, but metadata retrieval alone does not demonstrate content reading. Do not cite unrelated hits for padding, invent missing keys, or claim this stage read benchmark papers. Later source consultation must stay within the task'\\\"'s explicit exclusion boundary: exact allowed identifiers only, no citation-graph or related-content expansion, and stop/document accidental prohibited exposure. No external literature retrieval occurred in this framing stage. Do not assert first-ever novelty.\\n+\\n+Follow \\\"'`VENUE.md`: ICLR 2026, anonymous manuscript, no author identities, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, personal links, or internal absolute paths. Verify official template provenance and any actual venue checklist; do not import a checklist from another conference. Include `LLM Usage` after references.\\n+\\n+## Forbidden framing\\n+\\n+Avoid headings centered on **Search Progression**, **Campaign Phases**, **Milestones**, **Timeline**, **First Success**, **Experiment A / Experiment B**, **Cycle-by-Cycle Improvements**, **Winning Candidate**, and **Architecture Evolution**.\\n+\\n+Avoid phrases such as “we then tried,” “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “later variants,” “next-best,” and “search winner.” Do not claim “median is universally more robust,” “Borda is universally safer,” “measured training-data cost,” “proved contamination effectiveness,” “continuous cap frontier,” or state-of-the-art resistance.\\n+\\n+Internal labels banned from visible paper prose include `correctness rate`, `correct and eligible`, `eligible`, `combined_score`, `rubric`, `worker_01`, and `worker_02`. Their appearance in this memo is private audit guidance only. If a thresholded metric is used, give it one reader-facing meaning per figure; Figure 3 uses only reachability of co-first under the stated cap.\\n+\\n+## Stage completion criteria\\n+\\n+Validate the two strategy files for JSON shape, source-ID existence, consistent decisions, example/count grounding, figure budgets, and a nonempty grounded supplement. Do not draft `paper.tex` or `claims_grounding.json`, generate the figures, run new candidate experiments, or invoke final-paper validation in this stage.\\n*** End Patch\\nPATCH'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Success. Updated the following files:\\nA paper_strategy.json\\nA paper_strategy.md\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
