e File: scripts/build_results.py\n@@\n-    resources = {family: json.loads((ROOT / \\\"task/resources/final\\\" / (family + \\\".json\\\")).read_text())\n+    resources = {family: json.loads((ROOT / \\\"data\\\" / (family + \\\".json\\\")).read_text())\n*** Update File: scripts/reproduce_allocations.py\n@@\n-        resource = json.loads((ROOT / \\\"task/resources/final\\\" / (family + \\\".json\\\")).read_text())\n+        resource = json.loads((ROOT / \\\"data\\\" / (family + \\\".json\\\")).read_text())\n@@\n-                           \\\"@evidence[[task/resources/final/helm.json]]\\\",\n-                           \\\"@evidence[[task/resources/final/openllm.json]]\\\"], kind=\\\"derived\\\")\n+                           \\\"@evidence[[data/helm.json]]\\\",\n+                           \\\"@evidence[[data/openllm.json]]\\\"], kind=\\\"derived\\\")\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n The hypothesis is conditional: the ordering of task budgets need not survive a change in cap. This follows mechanistically from distinct sufficient statistics. Mean uses total added score mass; median uses central threshold crossings; Borda uses pairwise crossings against the whole competitor population. None of these mechanisms implies the same ranking of investment costs on every target vector.\n+\n+There is nevertheless a simple within-rule restriction. Holding the table and target fixed, a larger cap weakly expands the set of successful subsets. Thus, with infinity ordered above finite budgets,\n+\\\\begin{equation}\n+ \\\\rho_1\\\\leq\\\\rho_2\\\\quad\\\\Longrightarrow\\\\quad\n+ \\\\budget_r(t,\\\\rho_1)\\\\geq\\\\budget_r(t,\\\\rho_2).\n+ \\\\label{eq:monotone}\n+\\\\end{equation}\n+This implication follows from the same target--rival monotonicity used by the endpoint test. It does not order different rules: their budgets may increase by different amounts when the cap shrinks. Observing a reversal between rules therefore does not contradict monotonicity of either rule's own budget. This separation distinguishes the proposed comparison from an assertion that a smaller permitted improvement can make promotion cheaper.\n+% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L260]]\n+% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L193]]\n@@\n-\\\\section{LLM Usage}\n+\\\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\n)\\\"; /agent-tmp/paper-tools/bin/python scripts/stage_resources.py; for stem in helm openllm; do { printf '%s\\\\n' '*** Begin Patch' '*** Update File: paper.tex' '@@' \\\"-\\\\\\\\input{tables/\"'${stem}_complete.tex}\"; sed '\"'s/\"'^/+/'\"' tables/\"'${stem}_complete.tex; printf '\"'%s\\\\n' '*** End Patch'; } | (codex --codex-run-as-apply-patch \\\"\"'$(cat)\"); done; /agent-tmp/paper-tools/bin/python - <<'\"'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport subprocess\nfor name in ['paper.tex','scripts/build_claims.py']:\n text=Path(name).read_text(); updated=text.replace('task/resources/final/','data/')\n old_lines=text.splitlines();new_lines=updated.splitlines()\n import difflib\n diff=list(difflib.unified_diff(old_lines,new_lines,lineterm=''))\n patch='*** Begin Patch\\\\n*** Update File: '+name+'\\\\n'+'\\\\n'.join(diff[2:])+'\\\\n*** End Patch'\n if text\"'!=updated:subprocess.run(['\"'codex','--codex-run-as-apply-patch',patch],check=True)\nPY\nPATH=/agent-tmp/paper-bin:/agent-tmp/paper-tools/bin:\"'$PATH bash .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex > /agent-tmp/latex.log 2>&1; tail -3 /agent-tmp/latex.log'","aggregated_output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nM paper.tex\nSuccess. Updated the following files:\nM paper.tex\nFailed to find context '-94,7 +94,7 @@' in /workspace/paper/paper.tex\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 9, in <module>\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/subprocess.py\", line 571, in run\n    raise CalledProcessError(retcode, process.args,\nsubprocess.CalledProcessError: Command '['codex', '--codex-run-as-apply-patch', \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: paper.tex\\n@@ -94,7 +94,7 @@\\n \\n Allocation methods are compared with no intervention, marginal greedy selection, and a seeded random task order evaluated by successive prefixes. Marginal greedy chooses the task producing the largest current improvement in the target's gap to its strongest rival, with deterministic task-order ties. A random order is drawn for each decision, not optimized over many orders. These are selection policies under the same response model, not alternative training procedures. The original development comparator uses exhaustive allocation on small tables and greedy allocation otherwise; its selection score does not supply a matched final population comparison.\\n % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py#L59]]\\n-% @evidence[[task/resources/final/protocol.json#/baselines]]\\n+% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/baselines]]\\n % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study]]\\n \\n \\\\subsection{Scalar allocation certificates}\\n@@ -131,21 +131,21 @@\\n \\\\section{Public Score Tables and Comparison Protocol}\\n \\\\paragraph{Snapshots and preprocessing.}\\n The study uses HELM's MMLU release v1.13.0 and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot, acquired on 7 November 2026. HELM contributes 57 subject scores; Open LLM contributes six normalized leaderboard columns: IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-PRO. Open LLM scores are divided by 100; its additional raw columns are not treated as independent tasks. The comparison recomputes all three aggregation rules from these component scores. It is not a claim about the live ordering displayed by either website.\\n-% @evidence[[task/resources/final/helm.json#/tasks]]\\n-% @evidence[[task/resources/final/openllm.json#/tasks]]\\n-% @evidence[[task/resources/final/protocol.json#/normalization]]\\n+% @evidence[[data/helm.json#/tasks]]\\n+% @evidence[[data/openllm.json#/tasks]]\\n+% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/normalization]]\\n % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\\n \\n Score content and known model aliases are clustered before a deterministic content-based split separates development from final analysis. Exact duplicates are collapsed; normalization is applied after assignment. The frozen final competitor tables, not just the selected targets, determine every comparison. Known alias matching reduces exact overlap but cannot establish unseen-model-family independence because lineage information is incomplete. Appendix~\\\\ref{sec:repro} specifies versions, hashes, and the acquisition code.\\n \\n \\\\paragraph{Targets and matched comparisons.}\\n Each final score table has eight fixed targets, selected at rank-spaced positions among nonwinners under its original arithmetic mean. Target selection is not repeated separately for each rule. Consequently, a target can already be co-first under median or Borda. We retain such zero-budget cases. Each target is assessed under every rule at both positive caps, keeping all other inputs fixed. Target labels are frozen row indices, not model-family categories.\\n-% @evidence[[task/resources/final/protocol.json#/target_selection]]\\n+% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/target_selection]]\\n % @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/targets]]\\n \\n The records contain three seeded executions, with seeds 42011, 42013, and 42017, of the same target instances. There are 48 distinct target--rule--cap decisions per score table. Independent validation recomputes aggregate success and checks the reference bounds. The reference uses exhaustive subsets for the six-task table, sorting or threshold counting for scalar rules on HELM, and a time-limited binary optimization reference for HELM Borda. We report a finite optimum only where a valid witness and matching lower and upper bounds are recorded; unresolved optimization would remain a bound interval. Independently certified unattainability is kept separate from missing or failed computation.\\n % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\\n-% @evidence[[task/resources/final/protocol.json#/baselines]]\\n+% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/baselines]]\\n \\n \\\\paragraph{Checks and uncertainty.}\\n Task permutations, constant-score tables, and zero-update controls test implementation behavior and are excluded from the empirical target denominator. A recorded reset replay checks a repeated status/effort signature. Repeated seeds do not resample tasks or models. We therefore report deterministic paired differences and observed repeatability, not confidence intervals for an unspecified population. The random comparator's range reflects only its recorded orders. No statistical test is used to turn these fixed-panel counterexamples into a universal rule ranking.\\n@@ -389,9 +389,9 @@\\n The Open LLM contents revision is\\n \\\\begin{center}\\\\small\\\\texttt{9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2}.\\\\end{center}\\n The HELM source is the public bucket's MMLU release v1.13.0. The accompanying frozen resources retain upstream URLs, upstream byte checksums, original source-row indices, and known aliases. The acquisition code canonicalizes Unicode/whitespace and raw score content before split assignment. Raw content and known alias clusters are disjoint across the recorded split; unknown lineage is not covered by that check. HELM is already on a unit scale; Open LLM uses the published normalized columns divided by 100, without treating raw columns as additional tasks. This choice makes the response cap relative to the published task scale. A different nonlinear score calibration would define a different intervention problem.\\n-% @evidence[[task/resources/final/openllm.json#/rows/0/provenance/0]]\\n-% @evidence[[task/resources/final/helm.json#/rows/0/provenance/0]]\\n-% @evidence[[task/resources/final/protocol.json#/normalization]]\\n+% @evidence[[data/openllm.json#/rows/0/provenance/0]]\\n+% @evidence[[data/helm.json#/rows/0/provenance/0]]\\n+% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/normalization]]\\n \\n \\\\paragraph{Reproduction entry points.}\\n \\\\path{scripts/build_results.py} reads the immutable evidence registry and frozen resource dimensions. It generates Table~\\\\ref{tab:budgets}, both quantitative figures, the complete supplementary tables, \\\\path{derived/allocations.csv}, and \\\\path{derived/summary.json}. It does not rerun the evaluator. \\\\path{scripts/reproduce_allocations.py} independently checks recorded selected subsets against the score tables without changing any measured result. \\\\path{scripts/acquire_scores.py} preserves the supplied upstream acquisition procedure for reacquiring score snapshots into a separate destination. The complete commands, dependencies, checksums, and generation map are in \\\\path{REPRODUCIBILITY.md}. The conceptual overview is a separate deterministic rendering step owned by the document pipeline, not a measured plot.\\n*** End Patch\"]' returned non-zero exit status 1.\nOutput written on paper.pdf (15 pages, 278684 bytes).\nTranscript written on paper.log.\nValidated rendered Supplementary Information.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
